Figure 1: Examples of idempotent and non-idempotent instruction sequences in LLVM IR.
2.2
Idempotent Backward Slices
A program slice is a subset of a program’s statements that may affect a specific point in the program,
called the slice criterion. Early applications of program slicing, as proposed by Weiser [24], aimed to help
developers focus on relevant code during tasks such as debugging and testing. Later, the concept of slicing
proved useful in compiler optimizations such as auto-parallelization [12], redundancy elimination [21], and
the removal of cold code from critical paths [23].
The standard formalization of program slices, as presented by Blazy et al. [3], refers to dense slices.
Given a program P, a dense slice is a subprogram S ⊆P that determines the computation of an instruction
ι ∈P. In contrast, a sparse slice, as proposed by Rodrigues et al. [17], captures the computation of a value
in a program represented in Static Single Assignment (SSA) form [6]. This paper focuses on a variation of
sparse slices that we call idempotent slices, defined as follows:
Definition 2 (Idempotent Backward Slice). Let v be a variable defined in an SSA-form program P. The
idempotent backward slice S ⊆P with respect to v, the slice criterion, is a subset of instructions in P
satisfying the following properties:
Single-Entry: The set of basic blocks BS ⊆P that contain instructions in S forms a single-entry region in
P; that is, there exists a unique basic block b ∈BS such that every path from the entry of P to any
block in BS must first pass through b.
Idempotent: Let I be the set of free variables in S (its inputs). Multiple executions of S with the same
binding of values to variables of I always yield the same result (see Definition 1).
Example 2 illustrates some of the concepts introduced in Definition 2. The example mentions the Gated
Static Single-Assignment form, which Section 2.3 will revist in more detail.
Example 2. We use the program in Figure 2 (a) to illustrate the notion of idempotent slices. The semantics
of this program is immaterial for the discussion that follows. Figure 2 (b), adapted from Figure 2 (e) in the
work of Blazy et al. [3], shows a dense slice computed for the instruction s = s + 1 in line 10. Figure 2 (c)
shows the control-flow graph of the same program converted to SSA form. Part (d) of the figure depicts
the corresponding Gated Static Single-Assignment representation, in which ϕ-functions at loop headers are
replaced with µ-functions. Additionally, a η-function renames variable s1 into s4 as it exits the loop. Note
that the η-function is guarded by predicate p0, which controls the loop. Figure 2 (e) shows the backward
idempotent slice with respect to variable s3, and Figure 2 (f) shows the slice with respect to the criterion x2.
The value of s3 in Figure 2 (e) depends exclusively of the set of inputs {N, x0, s0}. Notice that s3 is defined
outside the loop; hence, regardless of this loop’s trip count, s3 is assigned only once. Similarly, the value of
x2 in Figure 2 (f) depends only from the initial bindings of the inputs x0 and x2. Notice that, in this case,
the backward slice does not escape the loop that encloses the slice criterion, otherwise x2 would be affected
multiple times.
Example 2 highlights an important distinction between the classic, i.e., dense, notion of a backward
slice and the idempotent backward slice introduced in this paper. A dense backward slice, as defined in the
original work of Weiser [24] and formalized by Blazy et al. [3], is a subset of labels in a program that affect
the slice criterion in the same way as the original program. An idempotent slice, in contrast, is a referentially
transparent function that defines a single value. As a result, a maximal backward slice must be restricted to
the loop in which its slice criterion is defined; otherwise, it would compute multiple values for that criterion:
one per loop iteration. For this reason, the slice of x2 in Figure 2 (f) contains only two basic blocks and
never escapes the loop where x2 is defined.
