2.3
Gated Static Single-Assignment Form
The Gated Static Single Assignment form [22, 13] mentioned in Example 2 extends the conventional Static
Single Assignment representation [6] by making explicit the control conditions that govern the flow of values
through a program.
In SSA, ϕ-functions merge values from multiple predecessors but omit the control
predicates that determine which value is chosen at runtime. GSA replaces these ϕ-functions with three kinds
of gate instructions—γ, µ, and η—which jointly encode data and control dependencies:
• µ-instructions: represent loop headers in GSA form. They replace ϕ-functions when the join point
corresponds to a loop entry. A µ-instruction expresses the recurrence of values across iterations by
associating each incoming value with the control predicate that enables the corresponding loop back
edge. Hence, µ-instructions make explicit the control dependencies governing value flow in iterative
constructs.
• γ-instructions: represent simple junction points. They generalize ϕ-functions by pairing each incom-
ing value with a gate—a predicate characterizing the control-flow path from the reaching definition
to the merge point. At runtime, the argument whose predicate evaluates to true is selected. Thus,
γ-instructions encode more information than ϕ-functions, as they explicitly represent both control and
data dependencies.
• η-instructions: represent value gating. They propagate values only under certain control conditions,
linking a value’s liveness to a control predicate. In Figure 2 (d), η-instructions ensure that values
4


produced within loops or conditional regions are properly guarded before being used or returned outside
those regions.
The GSA representation enables analyses and transformations that require joint reasoning about data
and control flow. As Section 3 will discuss, one such application is the computation of idempotent backward
slices.
2.4
Limitations of Previous Work to Compute Idempotent Slices
In 2023, Guimar˜aes and Pereira [10] proposed a fast algorithm to identify backward slices.
Their goal
was to transform function arguments into thunks; that is, lambda expressions that are evaluated lazily,
only when needed by the callee. To produce a thunk encoding the computation of an actual parameter p,
Guimar˜aes and Pereira outline the backward slice that calculates the value of p. In order to identify these
slices efficiently, they traverse the sparse dependence graph that produces the value p. For completeness,
Definition 3 formalizes the notion of a sparse backward dependence graph.
Definition 3 (Sparse Backward Dependence Graph). The dependence graph G = (V, E) relative to the
SSA variable v with program P is defined as follows:
Base: Variable v ∈V ;
Data: If B : v0 = v1 ⊕v2 is an instruction in basic block B ∈P, and v0 ∈V , then {v1, v2} ⊂V , and
{v1 →v0, v2 →v0} ⊆E;
Control: If B : br p B0 B1 is a branch that terminates basic block B ∈P, then for every instruction
B′ : v0 = . . . in basic block B′ ∈P, such that: (i) B dominates B′; (ii) B′ does not dominate B; and
(iii) v0 ∈V , we have that p ∈V and p →v0 ∈E.
Guimar˜aes and Pereira uses the sparse method introduced by Rodrigues et al. [17] to compute the
backward dependence graph of Definition 3. This algorithm, in turn, is based on the definition of control
dependence by Ferrante et al. [9]: a basic block Bs controls a basic block Bu if Bs dominates Bu, but Bu does
not post-dominate Bs. From this observation, Rodrigues et al. uses the four rules seen in Figure 3 (a) to infer
data and control dependencies. The algorithm of Rodrigues et al. traverses the program’s dominator tree,
stacking predicates that control branches. Upon visiting instructions that define new values, it creates control
dependencies between the predicate on the top of the stack and such values. However, when this notion,
originally defined for program labels, is transposed to SSA-form variables, Rodrigues et al.’s algorithm
misses certain control-dependency edges. In our reimplementation of Guimar˜aes and Pereira’s approach,
these missing edges proved necessary to correctly capture all branches in the extracted slice, as illustrated
in Example 3.
Example 3. Figure 3 (b) shows a simplified control-flow pattern that emerged from the compilation of
function reduceDB, from the minisat benchmark from the LLVM test suite1 once it is optimized with the
simplifycfg pass from the -Os flag.
Figure 3 (c) shows the backward dependency graph with regards
to criterion x3 that the algorithm proposed by Rodrigues et al. [17] builds. When producing the slice for
x3, Guimar˜aes and Pereira’s algorithm would miss the basic block B2. This happens because, according to
Ferrante et al. [9]’s definition of control dependencies, B2 does not control neither B3 or B4, nor B5, as it
does not dominate any of these nodes. Thus, when following this definition of control dependency, Rodrigues
et al.’s algorithm misses the fact that predicate p2 also controls the assignments into x0 and x1.
The pattern that Figure 3 (b) illustrates is very specific: we could identify it only once among 2007 programs
in the LLVM test suite. Nevertheless, its occurrence leads to an incorrect computation of backward depen-
dencies, following Rodrigues et al.’s algorithm. Section 3 shall present a new algorithm to compute sparse
backward slices that is correct under this scenario.
