3
Idempotent Backward Slices
This section presents an algorithm to identify idempotent backward slices that meet the properties listed in
Definition 2, and that overcome the limitations discussed in Section 2.4. We start explaining, in Section 3.2,
how such slices can be correctly identified. Then, in Section 4.1, we show how the slice can be outlined as a
stand-alone function.
3.1
From SSA to GSA
To build the GSA representation, we follow the algorithm of Tu and Padua [22], which adds gates (predicates)
to SSA-form programs. This conversion unifies the placement of ϕ-functions and the derivation of the control
predicates that guard their incoming values into a single, almost-linear-time process based on the concept of
a gating path. Given a ϕ-function located at a merge block B, a gating path with respect to this ϕ-function
satisfies the following informal properties:
1. It starts at the immediate dominator idom(B). The immediate dominator2 of B is the unique point
where control flow last converges before diverging toward B.
2. It corresponds to a unique sequence of branch predicates that determine which reaching definition flows
into B.
3. It ends at B, the block containing the ϕ-function.
At merge points, ϕ-functions are thus replaced by γ-functions, which explicitly encode the Boolean
predicates characterizing their gating paths. Example 4 illustrates this notion.
Example 4. Consider Figure 4(a).
The merge block B5 contains a ϕ-function for x2.
