By analyzing gating paths, we augment each ϕ-function with the corresponding Boolean guard so that
the selected value becomes explicit. In GSA, this produces γ-instructions, of the form x = γ(P, xtrue, xfalse),
meaning:
• if P evaluates to true, then x receives xtrue;
• otherwise, x receives xfalse.
Example 5. In Figure 4(b), the ϕ-function x2 = ϕ(x0 : B3, x1 : B4) is replaced by the γ-instruction
x2 = γ
 p0 ∧(p1 ∨p2), x0, x1
 
. Here, the guard p0 ∧(p1 ∨p2) summarizes all the conditions under which
control reaches B5 via B3. The negation of this condition covers the cases reaching B4. Thus, the γ-function
precisely captures the value selection previously implicit in SSA.
While µ- and γ-instructions are explicitly constructed by the algorithm of Tu and Padua [22], η-
instructions were introduced earlier by Ottenstein et al. [13] as part of the original definition of GSA.
7


An η-instruction such as v0 = η(vexit, p) appears in a block that exits a loop where vexit is defined and whose
iteration is controlled by predicate p. Introducing such instructions is straightforward, as they naturally
arise from the notion of loop-closed form.3
3.2
Slice Identification
Once a program has been converted into GSA form, we identify the set of variables that constitute its
idempotent backward slice (Definition 2) via a backward traversal over the program’s dependence graph
(Definition 3).
Under GSA, all dependences relevant to slicing are explicit in the syntax of instructions. Thus, unlike
classic slicing, there is no need to separately compute data and control dependences.
For instance, an
η-instruction x = η(p, xexit) introduces a control dependence p →x and a data dependence xexit →x.
Similarly, a γ-instruction x = γ(P, xtrue, xfalse) introduces two data dependences xtrue →x and xfalse →x,
plus one control dependence p →x for every predicate p ∈P.
Example 6. Figure 4(c) depicts the backward dependence graph for variable x3. Three incoming edges
to x2—namely p0 →x2, p1 →x2, and p2 →x2—correspond to control dependences; all others are data
dependences.
However, for slice extraction, this distinction is irrelevant: the backward traversal simply
follows all edges.
The traversal adopts two stop criteria; that is, it terminates when either of the following conditions holds:
Loop: A slice for criterion v cannot leave the loop in which v is defined.
Function: Slices are intra-procedural; they cannot cross function boundaries.
These conditions define the Stop Set of a slice for criterion v: the collection of variables whose incoming
edges do not need to be explored. Function parameters are natural stop points. They have no incoming
dependencies; thus, they satisfy the Function condition. To enforce the Loop condition, we assign each
GSA variable a loop depth, equal to the loop nesting depth of its definition. The backward traversal for a
criterion v stops when it reaches either: (i) a µ-instruction defining a variable of the same loop depth as v,
or (ii) any variable of smaller depth. This ensures that the slice remains local to the loop that defines v.
Example 7. Figure 5 shows the backward dependence graphs for variables s3, x2, and p0 (previously seen
in Figure 2(d)). Figure 5(a) reproduces the GSA program; part (b) annotates each variable with its loop
depth. Part (c) shows the dependence graph for s3, which has depth 0. The traversal reaches only function
parameters, which therefore form the stop set. Part (d) shows the graph for x2 (depth 1). The traversal
stops at x1, defined by a µ-instruction at the same depth. Finally, part (e) shows the graph for p0 (also
depth 1). The traversal stops both at the depth-1 definition of x1 and at N, a depth-0 parameter.
Definition 3, yields the complete and correct set of variables that integrate the backward slice, given that
each node represents a program statement or SSA variable, and each edge denotes either a data or a control
dependence.
4
Slice-Based Code-Size Reduction
We call Slice-Based Code-Size Reduction (SBCR) a compiler transformation that applies the following se-
quence of steps on a program P:
1. Identify a set S of idempotent backward slices in P (see Section 3);
2. Outline these slices into a set F of functions (see Section 4.1);
3. Identify a subset F ′ ⊆F of functions that compute the same slice (see Section 4.2);
4. Merge the functions in F ′, and replace their slices with a single call given a cost model that estimates
code-size reduction (see Section 4.3).
3In LLVM, the Loop-Closed SSA (LCSSA) form enforces that all values defined inside a loop are used only within that loop.
If a value is needed outside the loop, LCSSA inserts a unique ϕ-function at the loop exit to capture it. In our case, we augment
this ϕ-function with the predicate controlling the loop, as illustrated in Figure 2(d).
