4.1
Function Outlining
Once a slice has been identified, it can be extracted into a separate function through outlining.
This
transformation is guarded by a set of legality checks that ensure the slice is idempotent; that is, that it
causes no side effects and only reads from memory. If all checks succeed, outlining proceeds in four steps:
1. Region Identification: The set of basic blocks (Vs) identified by the backward traversal defines the
slice region to be extracted (Definition 4).
2. Interface Definition: The function interface is created. Its parameters are the elements of the slice’s
stop set, and its return type matches the type of the slicing criterion v.
3. Code Cloning: The basic blocks in the region are cloned into a new function.
A value map is
used to remap references: internal dependencies are redirected to the cloned instructions, and external
dependencies are replaced with the new function parameters.
4. Instruction Replacement: The original region in the parent function is removed and replaced with
a single call to the newly outlined slice function.
Definition 4 (Slice Region). Let G = (V, E) be a program’s control-flow graph, and let S be the set of
variables in the backward dependence graph for a slice criterion v. The Slice Region Vs ⊆V is the set of
basic blocks that contain at least one instruction defining a variable in S.
Recovering Control Flow in the Slice
Reconstructing the control-flow graph of the slice requires
rewriting branch instructions, because control-flow paths in the parent CFG may cross blocks outside Vs.
The reconstruction relies on the notion of first dominator, introduced by Guimar˜aes and Pereira [10] and
revisited below.
9


Definition 5 (First Dominator). Given a control-flow graph G = (V, E), a subset V ′ ⊆V , and a node
b0 ∈V ′, the first dominator of b0 in V ′ is the unique node b1 ∈V ′ such that: (i) b1 dominates b0 in G, and
(ii) for every other b2 ∈V ′, b2 ̸= b1, if b2 dominates b0, then b2 also dominates b1. Operationally, b1 is found
via a backward walk along G’s dominator tree starting from b0 and stopping at the first node in V ′.
While Guimar˜aes and Pereira rely on this concept, they do not show that a slice region always contains
a first dominator. In our case, this property follows directly from Lemma 4.14.
Lemma 4.1 (Single-Entry Region). Let an Idempotent Backward Slice S be constructed by a backward
dependence traversal from a criterion v, identifying a slice region Vs. Then, there exists a unique block
bentry ∈Vs that dominates every other block in Vs.
Given a CFG G = (V, E) and a slice region Vs ⊆V , the slice’s CFG edges Es are reconstructed using
two rules:
Transposition: If b0, b1 ∈Vs and b0 →b1 ∈E, then b0 →b1 ∈Es.
Attraction: If b1 ∈Vs, b0 →b1 ∈E, and b0 /∈Vs, then let b be the first dominator of b0 in Vs; insert b →b1
into Es.
Example 8. Figure 6 illustrates CFG reconstruction using transposition and attraction. The slice region
is Vs = {BB0, BB1, BB2, BB5, BB9}. Edges between nodes inside Vs are preserved directly (e.g., BB5 →BB9).
Other edges must be redirected. For example, BB6 →BB8 is removed because BB6 /∈Vs; it is replaced with
BB2 →BB8 since BB2 is the first dominator of BB6 within Vs.
Slices produced by backward dependence traversal followed by transposition and attraction form single-
entry functions and preserve the observable behavior of the original program over the slice criterion. This is
formalized by Theorem 4.2.
Theorem 4.2 (Semantic Soundness). Let P be a program, Iv be a slicing criterion defining variable v, and
let S = Slice(P, Iv) be the extracted slice with single entry Lentry. For any initial store σin:
If P, Iv ⊢⟨Lentry, σin⟩→∗σP
then S, Iv ⊢⟨Lentry, σin⟩→∗σS
and
σS[v] = σP [v].
4.2
Common Slice Identification and Elimination
The identification and elimination of common slices leverages logic already implemented in the LLVM com-
piler. In our current prototype, we outline every slice whose criterion is a binary instruction. Thus, if a
program contains O(N) such instructions, the outliner may initially generate O(N) slice functions, and run
a worst case O(N 2) operations, as stated in Theorem 4.35.
Theorem 4.3. Let N be the number of instructions in a given program. If the number of candidate slice
criteria is O(N), and the worst-case time complexity to perform both backward dependence traversal and
outlining for a single slice is O(N), then the total worst-case time complexity for the complete slice extraction
phase is O(N 2).
Once all candidate slices have been outlined, we detect and eliminate duplicates using LLVM’s standard
mergefunc pass. This pass operates in two phases:
Phase 1: Structural Hashing (Fast Filtering). Each outlined function is assigned a structural hash
based on its instructions, constants, and control-flow skeleton.
Functions with distinct hashes are
immediately classified as different. This step reduces what would be a quadratic number of pairwise
comparisons to near-linear grouping.
