No Technique Subsumes the Others
In the cases where the slice-based technique reduces code, it
significantly outperforms baselines. Figure 9 shows this performance for the 23 programs where our ap-
proach reduced both Instcount and .text size. In these cases, our technique (blue) provides substan-
tial reductions, while IROutliner (green) is minimal and FMSA (red) often increases size.
For example,
in IndirectAddressing-dbl.test, SBCR achieved a -9.32% reduction. IROutliner got -0.35%, and FMSA
increased size by 1.07%.
Figure 9 demonstrates that SBCR finds redundancies in optimized code that the other algorithms miss;
however, the opposite also happens. Figure 10 compares the impacts for the 57 programs where our approach
increased both .text size and LLVM IR instruction count. In ldecod.test, a clear example of this trade-off,
the slice-based reduction increased code size by 1.97%. The IROutliner, in turn, achieved a small -0.53%
reduction, while FMSA yielded a significant -2.5% reduction.
A Deeper Look into a Worst-Case Benchmark
