5.3
RQ3: Running Time
The three optimizations discussed in this section were primarily designed for code-size reduction rather than
performance improvement. Consequently, their impact on execution time is generally limited: across most
transformed programs, we observed no statistically significant differences. However, individual benchmarks
do exhibit notable speedups or regressions, depending on the applied transformation. This section analyzes
these variations in detail.
Discussion.
Across all benchmarks, SBCR’s effect on execution time is not statistically significant, with
a geometric mean runtime variation of 0.06% (Table 1). Even if we restrict observations to the programs
that were effectively transformed by SBCR, the vast majority of them showed no measurable performance
difference. Where performance degraded, SBCR increased runtime by 4.48% across 14 programs. This increase
is due to function call overhead. Similarly, FMSA and IROutliner saw increases of 19.96% and 6.67% in 6 and
29 programs, respectively.
16


We have also observed situations where the performance of the transformed benchmarks improved. SBCR
reduced execution time by -3.39% in 7 benchmarks, while FMSA achieved a -15.16% reduction in 6 benchmarks,
and IROutliner a -8.34% reduction in 27 benchmarks. We believe that such improvements are due to better
instruction cache locality, as we discuss in the rest of this section.
Instruction Cache Performance Analysis
We analyzed hardware performance counters for Global-
DataFlow-dbl, which had the greatest reduction in execution time, instruction count, and .text size.
Comparing the -Os baseline to the SBCR version reveals lower cache-miss rates, which we believe is due
to improved instruction locality and cache utilization.
In the baseline configuration, hardware counters
recorded 6027 branch loads and 2165 branch-load misses, alongside 1736 L1 instruction cache loads and 1163
misses. After applying SBCR, these values shifted to 5661 branch loads, 1870 branch-load misses, and 1011 L1
instruction cache loads with 600 misses. This benchmark, GlobalDataFlow-dbl, is an ideal case for SBCR, as
the optimization enhances both instruction and data locality. The L1 data cache miss ratio fell from 66.99%
to 59.35%. Control flow also became more predictable (branch miss ratio decreased from 35.92% to 33.03%),
showing improved memory efficiency without significant control-flow penalties.
5.4
RQ4: Compilation Time
Slice-based code-size reduction, as defined in Section 4, is a relatively expensive transformation: it requires a
backward dependence traversal for every binary instruction in a program, followed by the outlining of slices
into standalone functions and the merging of equivalent functions. Nevertheless, this section shows that the
associated overhead remains modest when compared to the overall cost of a full compilation pipeline.
Discussion.
On average, SBCR increases compilation time by 4.22%, computed as the geometric mean over
the 303 programs whose compilation times changed (either positively or negatively). Figure 11 shows how
compilation time varies across this universe of benchmarks. The remaining 1704 programs exhibited no
statistically significant variation in compilation time. The two baselines we compare against also introduce
compilation overhead, although to a lesser extent. FMSA increases compilation time by 2.06% on the 241
programs that experienced changes (considering exclusively this optimization; i.e., this is not the universe
of benchmarks analyzed in Figure 11). The IROutliner, in turn, increases compilation time by 2.48% over
258 affected programs.
