6
Related Work
This work spans multiple areas in the programming languages literature, including classical program analyses
and representations (e.g., GSA construction), as well as recent advances in code-size reduction. This section
reviews key contributions in these domains.
Program Slicing.
Program slicing is a longstanding research area originating from the seminal work of
Weiser [24]. As illustrated in Example 2, our notion of idempotent slices differs from traditional program
slices: rather than extracting subprograms, we construct referentially transparent functions. Consequently,
slice identification and extraction operate on SSA values rather than program statements,as Section 3 ex-
plains.
In this sense, the work most closely related to ours is due to Guimar˜aes and Pereira [10], who use a
variation of idempotent slices to transform call-by-value into call-by-name semantics. However, as discussed
in Section 2.4, both the definition of slices and the underlying algorithms are different. Their approach relies
on Ferrante et al. [9]’s formulation of control dependencies, which they identify on the SSA graph using the
sparse algorithm of Rodrigues et al. [17]. This approach may fail to recognize certain branches that influence
slice results. Our technique avoids these limitations by leveraging the explicit dependency structure of GSA
form.
GSA Construction.
The Gated Static Single-Assignment form was introduced by Ottenstein et al. [13],
building on earlier work by Ferrante et al. [9] and emerging definitions of SSA form [1, 5, 18]. The construction
algorithm adopted in Section 3.1 originates from Tu and Padua [22]; however, our implementation follows
the more recent formalization by Herklotz et al. [11]. Herklotz et al. provide a formal operational semantics
for GSA-form programs and describe how to introduce gates into ϕ-functions. We opted to follow Herklotz
et al.’s formulation because Tu and Padua did not explicitly model η- and µ-functions in the way required by
our slice-based approach. Nevertheless, none of this previous literature explores GSA form as a foundation
for slicing. To the best of our knowledge, this work is the first to employ GSA form as a systematic basis
for slice extraction.
Code-Size Reduction.
Reducing code size by removing redundancy has been extensively studied, mo-
tivating a large body of recent research. Since instruction counts do not fluctuate statistically, code-size
reduction has become a cornerstone of stochastic compiler optimization [7, 19, 2]. Our approach, however,
is entirely deterministic. However, most previous deterministic instruction-outlining techniques focus on
discovering common contiguous instruction sequences [4]. In contrast, slice-based code-size reduction can
identify non-contiguous regions containing branches or loops (as shown in Figure 2).
Techniques such as HyFM [16] and FMSA [15] are also capable of outlining non-sequential code. However,
neither can merge redundant code located within the same function. The LLVM IROutliner [14] can do
it, but it is not region-based; hence, cannot outline code spanning multiple blocks. As demonstrated in
Section 5, SBCR is neither a subset nor a superset of FMSA or IROutliner. Like IROutliner, it can outline
both intra- and inter-procedurally, but unlike either previous approach, it is region-based. By identifying
a slice criterion and including all instructions that it depends on, SBCR can extract more expressive and
semantically coherent code regions, even across control-flow boundaries.
7
Conclusion
This paper introduces the concept of Idempotent Backward Slices and presents a practical algorithm for
identifying them in Gated SSA form. We argue that idempotent slices represent a new and expressive unit of
redundancy: referentially transparent computations that can be extracted, duplicated, and invoked without
changing program behavior. Based on this insight, we propose SBCR, a semantics-preserving transformation
that outlines idempotent slices into single-entry functions, enabling precise and safe code-size reduction. Our
evaluation on more than 2,000 programs from the LLVM Test Suite demonstrates that:
• SBCR achieves code-size reductions competitive with state-of-the-art approaches, especially in the pres-
ence of fine-grained redundancy.
20


• No single technique is universally superior: SBCR, IROutliner, and FMSA uncover distinct optimization
opportunities.
• Although quadratic in theory, SBCR exhibits near-linear scaling in practice, making it viable in real-
world compilation pipelines.
We view this work as a first step toward exploring idempotent slices as a general abstraction for redun-
dancy elimination. A key challenge moving forward is preventing the transformation from increasing code
size when applied aggressively. This concern motivates continued refinement of profitability heuristics and
cost models, or integration with profile-guided optimization. These are directions that we leave open as
future work.
Data Availability Statement
The implementation of all the ideas discussed in this paper is publicly available at https://github.com/
lac-dcc/Daedalus, under the GPL 3.0 license. The experiments reported in Section 5 used LLVM 17,
chosen for compatibility with the FMSA’s patch, IROutliner, and our pass. We extended the mergefunc pass
from LLVM to make possible the merging of functions within our pass. The specific LLVM 17 build source
code is available at https://github.com/Casperento/llvm-project/tree/merge-functions-pass. The
experiments were executed via shell scripts developed for this study, publicly available at https://github.
com/Casperento/daedalus-dbg-toolkit. We provide a docker image to reproduce all the experiments.
The Dockerfile is located at https://github.com/lac-dcc/Daedalus/tree/main/artifact/docker.
