2) RTL Code Instrumentation: To enable precise debugging
and hardware execution logic tracking, we implement an auto-
mated instrumentation pass that transforms the raw Verilog into
a Design Under Test (DUT), which is then forwarded to Stage
3. The goal of this process is to capture the concrete execution
path and the instantaneous register states during simulation,
enabling the detection of deep functional divergences that
black-box testing cannot detect.
The instrumentation workflow proceeds as follows:
• The framework parses the LLM-generated Verilog into an


6
Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) to construct a Control Flow
Graph (CFG).
• Each branch within the CFG is assigned a unique identifier
(Bi). The framework then injects monitoring logic (e.g.,
display statements) at the end of each basic block.
• Finally, the framework identifies all the registers and
inserts display statements to log the state of every register
at each clock cycle.
