3 Balanced Exponential Growth (BEG)
We expect that an untreated cancer cell population in a nutrient-rich environment
with no density restrictions will grow exponentially. Experimental evidence suggests
that the proportion of the total cell count in a given cell cycle phase will eventually
settle to a constant steady-state as the population grows [36–40]. Thus, the population
will eventually settle to a balanced exponential growth (BEG) regime, in which the
total cell count grows exponentially, but the proportion of cells in each cell cycle
phase becomes constant. As the proportion of cells in a given cell cycle phase can be
measured via flow cytometry [9, 11], we will exploit BEG when fitting the PDE model
to experimental data.
