G. Evaluation of Redundancy Elimination
To quantify the physical impact of our redundancy elimina-
tion, we synthesized the generated RTL designs using Yosys
and compared the average gate counts before and after Stage
3. Table VI presents the results, broken down into Total Cells
(including registers) and Combinational Logic Cells.
For the VerilogEval datasets, which consist of diverse logic
problems, we observed a substantial reduction in total gate
count: 19.2% for the machine-generated set and 26.6% for
the human-curated set. This indicates that the initial LLM-
generated code often contains superfluous logic branches (e.g.,
redundant default cases or unreachable else blocks) that inflate
the circuit area without contributing to functionality. For the
RTLLM v2.0 dataset, which comprises complex and realistic
RTL design scenarios, it optimized 10.5% in total gate count
and 4.5% in combinational logic.
