better than DIP-VAE-I when K = 10 (Fig. 5(c)). In contrast, factor-VAE and vanilla VAE fail
to separate the underlying factors, with LDs exhibiting mixed associations across features. The
posterior distributions learned by factor-VAE and vanilla VAE remain close to the prior, yielding
near-zero KL divergence and indicating posterior collapse. β-VAE exhibits partial separation across
LDs and is able to discover some factor-related structure at the same β value and C = 0 that bfVAE
adopts. This observation confirms the importance of term T3 in Eq. (1). When K is over-specified,
as presented in Fig. 5(c), DIP-VAE-I over-picks informative dimensions with high false discovery
rates on informative LDs while bfVAE has a zero false discovery rate.
Consistent results are
observed for the DBSR-LS procedure (App. C).
We further compute the LSDI values based on the outputs of FVH-LT and DBSR-LS to compare
bfVAE and existing disentangled VAE formulations. In addition, given that FA15 is a synthetic
dataset with known ground-truth generative factors, we further computed the disentanglement
score in Higgins et al. (2017) (see Sec. 1.3 for details) As shown in Tab. 2, bfVAE consistently
14


achieves the highest LSDI scores, indicating the best structural separation among LDs. Similarly
in the case of the disentanglement score, bfVAE attains the highest score, followed by DIP-VAE-I.
Overall, these findings demonstrate that bfVAE, together with FVH-LT and DBSR-LS, reliably
uncovers disentangled and interpretable latent structure across dimensions.
