ting it as tuner, and setting 0 sweeps in the sweepingStrategy
object.
• The tuner can implement the identity function returning the
same populations it receives in input. Consequently, the tun-
ing phase is optional.
• Master optimizers and tuner can potentially be other Sweep-
ing* optimizers, allowing for complex composite strategies.
Figure S2:
UML class diagram that visually summarizes the main software
components used to implement the Sweeping* algorithm and their
dependencies.
The classes fall into several conceptual groups,
each playing a specific role in the framework. The core optimizer
classes that are the fundamental building blocks for optimization:
MOOptimizer (the base class for all SV MO optimizers, it extends
MOOptimizerWS to support MV optimization with warm start),
Sweeping* (a composite optimizer implementing MVMOOptimizer),
NSGA*WS (a warm-start version of the NSGA* algorithm, can be
used both as SV and MV optimizer inside the Sweeping* frame-
work).
Warm-start infrastructure (warm-starting allows optimiz-
ers to begin from existing populations, enabling pipelines): MOOp-
timizerWS (extends MOOptimizer to add an optimizeWS method
that accepts an initial population), MOOptimizerWSFactory and
NSGA*Factory (these factory classes create warm-start optimiz-
ers on demand, using the current populations).
Genetic algo-
rithm components, these classes determine how populations evolve
during optimization: GAStrategy (an abstract class encapsulating
GA-specific behaviors), two concrete implementations are shown
( GASBitList, bitlist-based feature encoding, GASSelector, MV
individuals composed of SV individuals, one per view), GASFac-
tory, GASFactoryBitList, GASFactorySelector (factories for creat-
ing GAStrategy instances tailored to the current population han-
dling strategy). MVMOOptimizer implementations with specific MV
composition strategies, these define how separate views are com-
bined to form MV individuals: Concatenated (concatenates SV fea-
ture sets into a single large feature set, possibly with probabilis-
tic feature retention in the lean configuration), Resampled (uses a
GA where each MV individual is formed by one SV individual per
view, handles MV structure through resampling rather than con-
catenation). Together, these classes form a modular and extensible
architecture for MV MO optimization.
The Sweeping Algorithm is based on wrapping and composing of
simpler SV algorithms. To develop this framework, the following
elements were needed.
• MO algorithms supporting “warm start”: the possibility to
start from a previous population. In this manner pipelines of
optimizers can be formed.
• Parts of the pipelines use SV solution parts and a resampling
strategy (see Section 1.1.3). To obtain this behavior, GA parts
must be customized on the initial population, which in turn
requires to create the optimizers on the fly using factory ob-
jects.
• To reduce the clutter, the parts of the GA algorithm that can
change are encapsulated in strategy objects (see Supplemen-
tary Section 1.1.4).
Supplementary Fig. S2 shows the main classes that are used to com-
pose the MV MO algorithm that is featured in our tests and their
dependencies. The classes will be described in the following.
1.1.2 Multi-objective optimizer with warm start
Algorithm 2 MOOptimizerWS abstract class definition
1: class MOOptimizerWS inherits MOOptimizer
2:
method optimizeWS(objectives, trainingData, pop)
3:
method optimize(objectives, trainingData)
4:
return optimizeWS(objectives, trainingData, ∅)
5:
end method
6: end class
7: class MOOptimizerWSFactory
8:
method createWS(pops)
9: end class
In order to compose multiple MO optimizers in a pipeline, we need
optimizers able to start from an initial population that is the re-
sult of a previous optimization.
The class MOOptimizerWS ex-
tends MOOptimizer allowing to warm-start the optimization pro-
cess with an initial population of solutions. Calling optimizeWS
with an empty set of solutions is equivalent to use the optimizer
without a warm start.
For reasons that will became obvious in
the following, in order to use SV optimizers as building blocks of
MV optimizers, there is the need to include the design scenarios
in which the inner workings of a SV optimizer, like for example
the genetic operators of a GA, are affected by an initial popula-
tion or even multiple initial populations. To take this into account
an MOOptimizerWSFactory class is provided. Its instantiations
can create MOOptimizerWS objects on the fly taking into account
initial populations. The case in which there is only one initial pop-
ulation is simply handled by passing a collection containing a single
population to the method createWS. The pseudocode definitions
of MOOptimizerWS and MOOptimizerWSFactory are in Sup-
plementary Algorithm 2.
1.1.3 Multi-view multi-objective optimizers
Algorithm 3 MVMOOptimizer abstract class definition
1: class MVMOOptimizer inherits MOOptimizerWS
2:
method optimizeMV(objectives, views, pops)
3:
method optimizeWS(objectives, trainingData, pop)
4:
return optimizeMV(
objectives, [trainingData], [pop])[0]
5:
end method
6: end class
7: class Concatenated inherits MVMOOptimizer
8:
method new(optimizerFactory, lean)
9:
fact ←optimizerFactory
10:
self.lean ←lean
11:
end method
12:
method optimizeMV(objectives, views, pops)
13:
cV iews ←concat(views)
14:
opt ←fact.create(pops)
15:
pop ←concatenatePops(lean, pops)
16:
pop ←opt.optimizeWS(objectives, cV iews, pop)
17:
return viewPopsFromConcatenated(pop)
18:
end method
19: end class
20: class Resampled inherits MVMOOptimizer
21:
method new(optimizerFactory)
22:
fact ←optimizerFactory
23:
end method
24:
method optimizeMV(objectives, views, pops)
25:
cV iews ←concat(views)
26:
opt ←fact.create(pops)
27:
pop ←opt.optimize(objectives, cV iews)
28:
return viewPopsFromSampling(pop)
29:
end method
30: end class
MVMOOptimizer objects are MOOptimizerWS objects that can
also be applied to MV situations. Optimization with a single view
is a special case of optimization with multiple views, and can be
adapted for a MV optimizer simply by calling the method for MV
optimization, optimizeMV, with a collection containing a single
view and another collection containing a single initial population.
For this work, we needed two implementations of MVMOOpti-
mizer: Concatenated and Resampled. These are two strategies
2


to handle MV scenarios by using SV algorithms internally. In Con-
catenated, the views are concatenated (and also the initial pop-
ulations, if the warm-start feature is used), forming a single view
with as features the union of the features of all the views, then a SV
algorithm with warm start is applied to the dataset resulting from
the concatenation. Afterwards, a result population for each view
is obtained by splitting each concatenated solution so that for each
view there is an individual with only the features of the concate-
nated individual that belong to that view. The initial population is
created by permuting randomly the populations of each view, then
concatenating the individuals at the same position.
In the lean
variant of Concatenated, after creating an individual by concate-
nation of an individual from each view, it is filtered by keeping each
feature with a probability equal to the proportion of its view in the
set of features of that individual. The Resampled MVMOOpti-
mizer also concatenates the views, but is radically different in the
way it handles the individuals. An MOOptimizerWSFactory cre-
ates an optimizer according to an initial population for each view,
so that the internal operations of the optimizer, like the creation of
the initial solutions, mutation and crossover operators, can depend
on the initial populations. Each individual of the Resampled opti-
mizer is formed by a union of a SV individual for each view. At the
end of the execution the result populations are formed by simply
separating each MV individual in its components and putting them
in the result collection of the corresponding view. The pseudocode
definitions of MVMOOptimizer, Concatenated, and Resampled
are in Supplementary Algorithm 3.
1.1.4 Genetic algorithm strategy
Algorithm 4 GAStrategy abstract class definition
1: class GAStrategy
2:
method featureImportance(objectives, trainData)
3:
method createNewIndividuals(featImp, num)
4:
method evaluate(pop, objectives, trainData)
5:
method sort(pop)
6:
method crossover(pop)
7:
method mutation(pop)
8:
method handleClones(pop, featImp)
9: end class
10: class GASBitlist inherits GAStrategy
11:
. . .
12: end class
13: class GASSelector inherits GAStrategy
14:
. . .
15: end class
The GAStrategy abstract class is used to encapsulate some aspects
of a GA (e.g. what mutation operator to use), so that they can be
polymorphically modified without having to redefine the main struc-
ture of the GA. A GAStrategy defines how to compute a feature
importance, how to create new individuals, how to evaluate them,
how to sort them in an order of preference, what kind of crossover
and mutation operators to use, and how to handle clones (indi-
viduals with the same set of features). For this work, we used two
kinds of GAStrategy: GASBitlist and GASSelector. They dif-
fer mainly in the way they represent the individuals. GASBitlist
conceptually represents individuals as lists of bits where each posi-
tion represents a feature and a “1” in a position indicates that the
feature at that position is active in the individual (in practice we
use sparse lists to save memory). For the experiments described in
this work, the operations for GASBitlist are implemented as in
the NSGA3-CHS MO optimizer instantiation previously described
in Cattelani and Fortino (2024). GASSelector manages MV in-
dividuals that are composed by SV individuals, one for each view.
When a GASSelector object is instantiated it memorizes a vocab-
ulary of individuals for each view. When a new MV individual is
created, a random SV individual is extracted from each vocabulary.
The mutation operator can switch a single view individual with an-
other one (with a probability of 1/|views| in our experiments). The
crossover operator forms a new MV individual by extracting the SV
individuals from the MV parents, with equal probability. Feature
importance is not used (calling the method just returns the uniform
distribution). The pseudocode definition of GAStrategy, and the
signatures of GASBitlist and GASSelector, are in Supplemen-
tary Algorithm 4.
Algorithm 5 GASFactory abstract class definition
1: class GASFactory
2:
method create(pops)
3: end class
4: class GASFactoryBitlist inherits GASFactory
5:
. . .
6: end class
7: class GASFactorySelector inherits GASFactory
8:
. . .
9: end class
GASFactory objects are used to create GAStrategy objects
on the fly, using current information about the population of solu-
tions for each view. In the context of this work, we used two types
of GASFactory: GASFactoryBitlist to create GASBitlist ob-
jects, and GASFactorySelector to create GASSelector objects.
The constructor methods of the GASFactory concrete classes re-
ceive and memorize all the necessary parameters, like the crossover
and mutation frequencies or the sorting strategy. This code is omit-
ted here, we refer the interested readers to the source code public
repository. The pseudocode definition of GASFactory, and the sig-
natures of GASFactoryBitlist and GASFactorySelector, are
in Supplementary Algorithm 5.
1.1.5 NSGA* with warm start
Algorithm 6 NSGA*WS class definition
1: class NSGA*WS inherits MOOptimizerWS
2:
method new(nGenerations, popsize, tournament,
gaStrategy, hofFactory)
3:
self.nGenerations ←nGenerations
4:
self.popsize ←popsize
5:
self.tournament ←tournament
6:
gas ←gaStrategy
7:
self.hofFactory ←hofFactory
8:
end method
9:
method optimizeWS(objectives, trainData, pop)
10:
hof ←hofFactory.createNewHallOfFame
11:
featImp ←gas.featureImportance(
objectives, trainData)
12:
pop[|pop| : popsize] ←
gas.createNewIndividuals(
featImp, popsize −pop)
13:
for 0 : nGenerations do
14:
pop ←gas.evaluate(pop, objectives, trainData)
15:
pop ←gas.sort(pop)
16:
offspring ←tournament(pop)
17:
offspring ←gas.crossover(offspring)
18:
offspring ←gas.mutation(offspring)
19:
pop ←concat(pop, offspring)
20:
pop ←gas.handleClones(pop, featImp)
21:
pop ←gas.evaluate(pop, objectives, trainData)
22:
pop ←gas.sort(pop)
23:
hof.update(pop)
24:
pop ←pop[1..popsize]
25:
end for
26:
return hof
27:
end method
28: end class
The NSGA* algorithm has been introduced in Cattelani and Fortino
(2024). For easy of reference, we offer a rewriting of NSGA* with
the pseudocode style used in this work in Supplementary Section
1.2. NSGA* with warm start (NSGA*WS) is a generalization of
NSGA* that allows to use an initial population that is provided
from outside the algorithm. This is an essential feature to partici-
pate in pipelines of MO optimizers. NSGA*WS receives an memo-
rizes at construction all the required hyperparameters, including a
GAStrategy object. This encapsulates the details of some opera-
tions, like e.g. the mutation operator, so that they can be changed
without redefining the whole algorithm.
The GAStrategy also
handles the representation of the individuals. Having the possibil-
ity to run NSGA* with different representations for the individuals
is crucial for the MV approach presented here. During construction,
an NSGA*WS also receives a factory object for hall of fames. A
hall of fame is an observer of the population during the generations,
that updates an internal collection of preferred individuals and re-
turns it at the end of the optimization. To build the pipelines used
3


in this work, more than one kind of hall of fame is needed. When
the optimization starts (method optimizeWS) the algorithm asks
to the hall of fame factory object to instantiate a new hall of fame,
that will then be used throghout the generations. It also uses the
GAStrategy to create new individuals as needed to fill the popu-
lation up to the required popsize. This way it is not necessary to
pass from outside an initial population of cardinality popsize, and
the scenario without initial population is handled as a special case.
If the passed population is bigger than popsize it will be cropped
at the end of the first generation when the best popsize individ-
uals are selected.
The pseudocode definition of NSGA*WS is in
Supplementary Algorithm 6.
