pplementary materials and methods 1.1 Multi-view algorithm Figure S1: Schematic representation of the Sweeping* algorithm. The algo- rithm begins with a set of initial solutions, which may be pro- vided by the user or empty (in which case the algorithm starts from scratch). These solutions form the starting populations for each view. The user specifies objectives, i.e., evaluation met- rics (functions mapping a solution to a numerical value, possibly data-dependent), and views, where each view corresponds to a fea- ture subset representing a data modality for the same sample set. These define the problem the algorithm will optimize. A sweep is the main unit of iteration. Each sweep consists of: a SV step, “for each view →optimize view” (for every view, an SV optimizer with warm start is applied), and a MV step, “master” optimizer (after updating all views individually, master MV optimizer jointly op- timizes across all views). The SV →MV cycle is repeated for a user-defined number of sweeps. After all sweeps are completed, a tuner MV optimizer further refines the final MV population. The tuner may also be the identity function, making this phase optional. This final MV optimization step produces the resulting solutions. In this subsection, the Sweeping* algorithm gets formally defined. We define also an extension of NSGA*, NSGA* with Warm Start (NSGA*WS) that makes it usable as inner MV optimizer for Sweep- ing* by allowing it to start from a previously defined set of solutions. This allows the chaining of multiple optimization phases as needed by Sweeping*. A schematic representation of the Sweeping* algo- rithm is available in Supplementary Fig. S1, while an introductory description of the algorithm is in Section 2. Given a fixed set of samples observed through multiple views (modalities) and a set of user-defined objectives, Sweeping* searches for MV solutions that achieve good trade-offs across those objec- tives. The algorithm alternates between (i) optimizing within each view separately and (ii) optimizing across all views jointly. Sweeping* starts from a set of initial solutions (this set can be empty to start from scratch), and requires a user provided set of objectives and of views. An objective is a function from a solution and a set of samples to a numeric value, i.e. it is a definition of an evaluation metric that can potentially depend on a set of data- points. A view has a set of features and assigns at every sample a value for each of its features. All the views must have the same set of samples. A population of MV solutions is represented by a sequence of SV solutions for each view. After a MV optimization the SV solutions at the same position in the sequences are consid- ered as part of the same MV solution, i.e. the MV solution has a feature set that is the union of the features of the corresponding SV solutions. After the SV step, instead, the order of the SV solutions in their sequence has no meaning. While optimizing, Sweeping* has two phases: sweeping and tuning. During the sweeping phase, the optimizer performs a user-defined number of sweeps each composed of a SV and a MV steps. In the SV step, for each view, a SV MO optimizer optimizes the population of solutions starting from its previous composition. In the MV step, a MV optimizer considers all the views at the same time, and builds a population of MV solu- tions starting from the SV solutions of the previous step. This new MV population is optimized, then either passed back to start a new SV step or used as input for the tuning phase after the last sweep. The tuning phase applies an additional MV optimizer to the output of the sweeping phase. The output of the tuner algorithm is used as the result of the whole Sweeping* algorithm. Every optimizer can implement the identity function (return the initial population) so every part of Sweeping* is in fact optional. Which SV algorithms to use for each view and each sweep, which master algorithm to use for each sweep, and which tuning algorithm to run at the end, are user-defined hyperparameters. 1.1.1 Sweeping* Algorithm 1 Sweeping* 1: class Sweeping* inherits MVMOOptimizer 2: method new(sweepingStrategy) 3: self.sweepingStrategy ←sweepingStrategy 4: end method 5: method optimizeMV(objectives, views, pops) 6: for s in sweepingStrategy.sweeps do 7: for i in 0 : |views| do 8: pops[i] ←s.optimizers[i].optimizeWS( objectives, views[i], pops[i]) 9: end for 10: pops ←s.master.optimizeMV( objectives, views, pops) 11: end for 12: tuner ←sweepingStrategy.tuner 13: return tuner.optimizeMV(objectives, views, pops) 14: end method 15: end class The Sweeping* is an MVMOOptimizer (Supplementary Section 1.1.3) that is customized at creation with a sweepingStrategy ob- ject. While optimizing, Sweeping* has two phases: sweeping and tuning. During the sweeping phase, the optimizer performs a num- ber of sweeps as defined in the sweepingStrategy. For each sweep, there are a SV and a MV steps. In the SV step, for each view, a SV MO optimizer with warm start (MOOptimizerWS, Supple- mentary Section 1.1.2) optimizes the population starting from its previous composition. In the MV step, a MV optimizer is used to try to improve all the populations considered at the same time us- ing all of views. After all the sweeps have been performed, a tuning MV optimizer may be executed, using the current populations as its initial ones. Which SV algorithms to use for each view and each sweep, which master algorithm to use for each sweep, and which tuning algorithm to run at the end, are hyperparameters specified through the sweepingStrategy object. A pseudocode definition of the Sweeping* algorithm is in Supplementary Algorithm 1. Sweep- ing* has the following properties. • It accepts initial populations in input, but can also start from scratch if empty initial populations are passed. • It is a generalization of any MVMOOptimizer, since the be- haviour of any MVMOOptimizer can be reproduced by set- ∗Corresponding Author, e-mail: luca.cattelani@uef.fi, https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4852-2310 †Corresponding Author, e-mail: vittorio.fortino@uef.fi, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8693-5285 1 ting it as tuner, and setting 0 sweeps in the sweepingStrategy object. • The tuner can implement the identity function returning the same populations it receives in input. Consequently, the tun- ing phase is optional. • Master optimizers and tuner can potentially be other Sweep- ing* optimizers, allowing for complex composite strategies. Figure S2: UML class diagram that visually summarizes the main software components used to implement the Sweeping* algorithm and their dependencies. The classes fall into several conceptual groups, each playing a specific role in the framework. The core optimizer classes that are the fundamental building blocks for optimization: MOOptimizer (the base class for all SV MO optimizers, it extends MOOptimizerWS to support MV optimization with warm start), Sweeping* (a composite optimizer implementing MVMOOptimizer), NSGA*WS (a warm-start version of the NSGA* algorithm, can be used both as SV and MV optimizer inside the Sweeping* frame- work). Warm-start infrastructure (warm-starting allows optimiz- ers to begin from existing populations, enabling pipelines): MOOp- timizerWS (extends MOOptimizer to add an optimizeWS method that accepts an initial population), MOOptimizerWSFactory and NSGA*Factory (these factory classes create warm-start optimiz- ers on demand, using the current populations). Genetic algo- rithm components, these classes determine how populations evolve during optimization: GAStrategy (an abstract class encapsulating GA-specific behaviors), two concrete implementations are shown ( GASBitList, bitlist-based feature encoding, GASSelector, MV individuals composed of SV individuals, one per view), GASFac- tory, GASFactoryBitList, GASFactorySelector (factories for creat- ing GAStrategy instances tailored to the current population han- dling strategy). MVMOOptimizer implementations with specific MV composition strategies, these define how separate views are com- bined to form MV individuals: Concatenated (concatenates SV fea- ture sets into a single large feature set, possibly with probabilis- tic feature retention in the lean configuration), Resampled (uses a GA where each MV individual is formed by one SV individual per view, handles MV structure through resampling rather than con- catenation). Together, these classes form a modular and extensible architecture for MV MO optimization. The Sweeping Algorithm is based on wrapping and composing of simpler SV algorithms. To develop this framework, the following elements were needed. • MO algorithms supporting “warm start”: the possibility to start from a previous population. In this manner pipelines of optimizers can be formed. • Parts of the pipelines use SV solution parts and a resampling strategy (see Section 1.1.3). To obtain this behavior, GA parts must be customized on the initial population, which in turn requires to create the optimizers on the fly using factory ob- jects. • To reduce the clutter, the parts of the GA algorithm that can change are encapsulated in strategy objects (see Supplemen- tary Section 1.1.4). Supplementary Fig. S2 shows the main classes that are used to com- pose the MV MO algorithm that is featured in our tests and their dependencies. The classes will be described in the following. 1.1.2 Multi-objective optimizer with warm start Algorithm 2 MOOptimizerWS abstract class definition 1: class MOOptimizerWS inherits MOOptimizer 2: method optimizeWS(objectives, trainingData, pop) 3: method optimize(objectives, trainingData) 4: return optimizeWS(objectives, trainingData, ∅) 5: end method 6: end class 7: class MOOptimizerWSFactory 8: method createWS(pops) 9: end class In order to compose multiple MO optimizers in a pipeline, we need optimizers able to start from an initial population that is the re- sult of a previous optimization. The class MOOptimizerWS ex- tends MOOptimizer allowing to warm-start the optimization pro- cess with an initial population of solutions. Calling optimizeWS with an empty set of solutions is equivalent to use the optimizer without a warm start. For reasons that will became obvious in the following, in order to use SV optimizers as building blocks of MV optimizers, there is the need to include the design scenarios in which the inner workings of a SV optimizer, like for example the genetic operators of a GA, are affected by an initial popula- tion or even multiple initial populations. To take this into account an MOOptimizerWSFactory class is provided. Its instantiations can create MOOptimizerWS objects on the fly taking into account initial populations. The case in which there is only one initial pop- ulation is simply handled by passing a collection containing a single population to the method createWS. The pseudocode definitions of MOOptimizerWS and MOOptimizerWSFactory are in Sup- plementary Algorithm 2. 1.1.3 Multi-view multi-objective optimizers Algorithm 3 MVMOOptimizer abstract class definition 1: class MVMOOptimizer inherits MOOptimizerWS 2: method optimizeMV(objectives, views, pops) 3: method optimizeWS(objectives, trainingData, pop) 4: return optimizeMV( objectives, [trainingData], [pop])[0] 5: end method 6: end class 7: class Concatenated inherits MVMOOptimizer 8: method new(optimizerFactory, lean) 9: fact ←optimizerFactory 10: self.lean ←lean 11: end method 12: method optimizeMV(objectives, views, pops) 13: cV iews ←concat(views) 14: opt ←fact.create(pops) 15: pop ←concatenatePops(lean, pops) 16: pop ←opt.optimizeWS(objectives, cV iews, pop) 17: return viewPopsFromConcatenated(pop) 18: end method 19: end class 20: class Resampled inherits MVMOOptimizer 21: method new(optimizerFactory) 22: fact ←optimizerFactory 23: end method 24: method optimizeMV(objectives, views, pops) 25: cV iews ←concat(views) 26: opt ←fact.create(pops) 27: pop ←opt.optimize(objectives, cV iews) 28: return viewPopsFromSampling(pop) 29: end method 30: end class MVMOOptimizer objects are MOOptimizerWS objects that can also be applied to MV situations. Optimization with a single view is a special case of optimization with multiple views, and can be adapted for a MV optimizer simply by calling the method for MV optimization, optimizeMV, with a collection containing a single view and another collection containing a single initial population. For this work, we needed two implementations of MVMOOpti- mizer: Concatenated and Resampled. These are two strategies 2 to handle MV scenarios by using SV algorithms internally. In Con- catenated, the views are concatenated (and also the initial pop- ulations, if the warm-start feature is used), forming a single view with as features the union of the features of all the views, then a SV algorithm with warm start is applied to the dataset resulting from the concatenation. Afterwards, a result population for each view is obtained by splitting each concatenated solution so that for each view there is an individual with only the features of the concate- nated individual that belong to that view. The initial population is created by permuting randomly the populations of each view, then concatenating the individuals at the same position. In the lean variant of Concatenated, after creating an individual by concate- nation of an individual from each view, it is filtered by keeping each feature with a probability equal to the proportion of its view in the set of features of that individual. The Resampled MVMOOpti- mizer also concatenates the views, but is radically different in the way it handles the individuals. An MOOptimizerWSFactory cre- ates an optimizer according to an initial population for each view, so that the internal operations of the optimizer, like the creation of the initial solutions, mutation and crossover operators, can depend on the initial populations. Each individual of the Resampled opti- mizer is formed by a union of a SV individual for each view. At the end of the execution the result populations are formed by simply separating each MV individual in its components and putting them in the result collection of the corresponding view. The pseudocode definitions of MVMOOptimizer, Concatenated, and Resampled are in Supplementary Algorithm 3. 1.1.4 Genetic algorithm strategy Algorithm 4 GAStrategy abstract class definition 1: class GAStrategy 2: method featureImportance(objectives, trainData) 3: method createNewIndividuals(featImp, num) 4: method evaluate(pop, objectives, trainData) 5: method sort(pop) 6: method crossover(pop) 7: method mutation(pop) 8: method handleClones(pop, featImp) 9: end class 10: class GASBitlist inherits GAStrategy 11: . . . 12: end class 13: class GASSelector inherits GAStrategy 14: . . . 15: end class The GAStrategy abstract class is used to encapsulate some aspects of a GA (e.g. what mutation operator to use), so that they can be polymorphically modified without having to redefine the main struc- ture of the GA. A GAStrategy defines how to compute a feature importance, how to create new individuals, how to evaluate them, how to sort them in an order of preference, what kind of crossover and mutation operators to use, and how to handle clones (indi- viduals with the same set of features). For this work, we used two kinds of GAStrategy: GASBitlist and GASSelector. They dif- fer mainly in the way they represent the individuals. GASBitlist conceptually represents individuals as lists of bits where each posi- tion represents a feature and a “1” in a position indicates that the feature at that position is active in the individual (in practice we use sparse lists to save memory). For the experiments described in this work, the operations for GASBitlist are implemented as in the NSGA3-CHS MO optimizer instantiation previously described in Cattelani and Fortino (2024). GASSelector manages MV in- dividuals that are composed by SV individuals, one for each view. When a GASSelector object is instantiated it memorizes a vocab- ulary of individuals for each view. When a new MV individual is created, a random SV individual is extracted from each vocabulary. The mutation operator can switch a single view individual with an- other one (with a probability of 1/|views| in our experiments). The crossover operator forms a new MV individual by extracting the SV individuals from the MV parents, with equal probability. Feature importance is not used (calling the method just returns the uniform distribution). The pseudocode definition of GAStrategy, and the signatures of GASBitlist and GASSelector, are in Supplemen- tary Algorithm 4. Algorithm 5 GASFactory abstract class definition 1: class GASFactory 2: method create(pops) 3: end class 4: class GASFactoryBitlist inherits GASFactory 5: . . . 6: end class 7: class GASFactorySelector inherits GASFactory 8: . . . 9: end class GASFactory objects are used to create GAStrategy objects on the fly, using current information about the population of solu- tions for each view. In the context of this work, we used two types of GASFactory: GASFactoryBitlist to create GASBitlist ob- jects, and GASFactorySelector to create GASSelector objects. The constructor methods of the GASFactory concrete classes re- ceive and memorize all the necessary parameters, like the crossover and mutation frequencies or the sorting strategy. This code is omit- ted here, we refer the interested readers to the source code public repository. The pseudocode definition of GASFactory, and the sig- natures of GASFactoryBitlist and GASFactorySelector, are in Supplementary Algorithm 5. 1.1.5 NSGA* with warm start Algorithm 6 NSGA*WS class definition 1: class NSGA*WS inherits MOOptimizerWS 2: method new(nGenerations, popsize, tournament, gaStrategy, hofFactory) 3: self.nGenerations ←nGenerations 4: self.popsize ←popsize 5: self.tournament ←tournament 6: gas ←gaStrategy 7: self.hofFactory ←hofFactory 8: end method 9: method optimizeWS(objectives, trainData, pop) 10: hof ←hofFactory.createNewHallOfFame 11: featImp ←gas.featureImportance( objectives, trainData) 12: pop[|pop| : popsize] ← gas.createNewIndividuals( featImp, popsize −pop) 13: for 0 : nGenerations do 14: pop ←gas.evaluate(pop, objectives, trainData) 15: pop ←gas.sort(pop) 16: offspring ←tournament(pop) 17: offspring ←gas.crossover(offspring) 18: offspring ←gas.mutation(offspring) 19: pop ←concat(pop, offspring) 20: pop ←gas.handleClones(pop, featImp) 21: pop ←gas.evaluate(pop, objectives, trainData) 22: pop ←gas.sort(pop) 23: hof.update(pop) 24: pop ←pop[1..popsize] 25: end for 26: return hof 27: end method 28: end class The NSGA* algorithm has been introduced in Cattelani and Fortino (2024). For easy of reference, we offer a rewriting of NSGA* with the pseudocode style used in this work in Supplementary Section 1.2. NSGA* with warm start (NSGA*WS) is a generalization of NSGA* that allows to use an initial population that is provided from outside the algorithm. This is an essential feature to partici- pate in pipelines of MO optimizers. NSGA*WS receives an memo- rizes at construction all the required hyperparameters, including a GAStrategy object. This encapsulates the details of some opera- tions, like e.g. the mutation operator, so that they can be changed without redefining the whole algorithm. The GAStrategy also handles the representation of the individuals. Having the possibil- ity to run NSGA* with different representations for the individuals is crucial for the MV approach presented here. During construction, an NSGA*WS also receives a factory object for hall of fames. A hall of fame is an observer of the population during the generations, that updates an internal collection of preferred individuals and re- turns it at the end of the optimization. To build the pipelines used 3 in this work, more than one kind of hall of fame is needed. When the optimization starts (method optimizeWS) the algorithm asks to the hall of fame factory object to instantiate a new hall of fame, that will then be used throghout the generations. It also uses the GAStrategy to create new individuals as needed to fill the popu- lation up to the required popsize. This way it is not necessary to pass from outside an initial population of cardinality popsize, and the scenario without initial population is handled as a special case. If the passed population is bigger than popsize it will be cropped at the end of the first generation when the best popsize individ- uals are selected. The pseudocode definition of NSGA*WS is in Supplementary Algorithm 6. 1.1.6 NSGA* with warm start factory Algorithm 7 NSGA*Factory abstract class definition 1: class NSGA*Factory inherits MOOptimizerWSFactory 2: method new(nGenerations, popsize, tournament, gasFactory, hofFactory) 3: self.nGenerations ←nGenerations 4: self.popsize ←popsize 5: self.tournament ←tournament 6: self.gasFactory ←gasFactory 7: self.hofFactory ←hofFactory 8: end method 9: method create(pops) 10: gas ←gasFactory.create(pops) 11: return new NSGA*WS( nGenerations, popsize, tournament, gas, hofFactory) 12: end method 13: end class As seen previously, MOOptimizerWSFactory objects are needed to construct MO optimizers taking into account the current popu- lation. They must contain information about the desired hyperpa- rameters. Supplementary Algorithm 7 defines the NSGA*Factory factory class for NSGA*WS objects. 1.2 NSGA* Algorithm 8 MOOptimizer abstract class definition 1: class MOOptimizer 2: method optimize(objectives, trainingData) 3: end class Algorithm 9 Nsga* class definition 1: class NSGA* inherits MOOptimizer 2: method new( nGenerations, popsize, featureImportance, sort, tournament, mutation, cloneRepurposing) 3: self.nGenerations ←nGenerations 4: self.popsize ←popsize 5: self.featureImportance ←featureImportance 6: self.sort ←sort 7: self.tournament ←tournament 8: self.mutation ←mutation 9: self.cloneRepurposing ←cloneRepurposing 10: end method 11: method optimize(objectives, trainingData) 12: hof ←createNewHallOfFame 13: featImp ←self.featureImportance(trainingData) 14: pop ←createNewRandomIndividuals( featImp, self.popsize) 15: for 1 : self.nGenerations do 16: pop ←evaluate(pop, objectives) 17: pop ←self.sort(pop) 18: offspring ←self.tournament(pop) 19: offspring ←crossover(offspring) 20: offspring ←self.mutation(offspring) 21: pop ←concat(pop, offspring) 22: if self.cloneRepurposing then 23: pop ←replaceClonesWithNewIndividuals( pop, featImp) 24: end if 25: pop ←evaluate(pop, objectives) 26: hof ←updateHallOfFame(hof, pop) 27: pop ←self.sort(pop) 28: pop ←pop[1..self.popsize] 29: end for 30: end method 31: end class NSGA* has been introduced in Cattelani and Fortino (2024). In- Supplementary Algorithm 9, we provide a rewriting of NSGA* with the pseudocode style used in this work. Similarly, Supplementary Algorithm 8 is a rewriting of the interface MultiObjectiveOpti- mizer from the same work, now shortened to MOOptimizer. 1.3 Sweeping* algorithm setups In our benchmark, we compared five different setups of the Sweep- ing* algorithm. When an SV optimizer is required, the algo- rithm always employs the NSGA3-CHS inner optimizer (Cattelani and Fortino 2024), using a population size of 500 and three in- ner folds for evaluating the individuals. All other hyperparame- ters follow those in Cattelani and Fortino (2024). The Sweeping* algorithm is introduced in Supplementary Section 1.1.1, and the five configurations considered in this work are listed in Table S1. Each configuration has a full name and a shorter nickname used throughout the manuscript. The differences between the resampled, lean-concatenated, and non-lean-concatenated master steps are de- scribed in Supplementary Section 1.1.3. Sweeping generations are expressed as sequences that indicate the number of generations per- formed in each sweep. For every sweep, the specified number of generations is executed in both the SV and MV steps (see Sup- plementary Section 1.1.1). When a tuning phase is included, it is always performed using a concatenated NSGA3-CHS optimizer. Since each sweep contains both SV and MV steps, the total number of generations for every configuration is always 300. 1.4 Case study The considered cancer types are kidney renal clear cell carcinoma (KIRC), brain lower grade glioma (LGG), and sarcoma (SARC). For each cancer type we included the same three distinct data lay- ers from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) project (Hutter and Zenklusen 2018) as preprocessed in Wissel et al. (2023): clinical information, mRNA expression, and miRNA transcription profiles. The clinical features are age, gender, race, and tumor stage. The number of samples for KIRC, LGG, and SARC are respectively 312, 411, and 194. The three TCGA cohorts represent distinct survival signal regimes that influence the feasibility of MO feature selection (Supplementary Fig. S3). TCGA-LGG shows early and abundant 4 Table S1: Considered configurations of the Sweeping* algorithm. name nick master sweeping generations tuning generations concatenated [] 300 resampled sweeping Sw Resampled [50, 50, 50] 0 concatenated sweeping CSw Concatenated(lean=false) [150] 0 lean concatenated sweeping LCSw Concatenated(lean=true) [150] 0 resampled sweeping with tuning SwT Resampled [50, 50] 100 Figure S3: Event accumulation and censoring patterns across TCGA survival cohorts. Stacked area plots show cumulative numbers of deceased, censored, and surviving individuals over follow-up time for TCGA-KIRC, TCGA-LGG, and TCGA-SARC. The three cohorts display distinct survival data regimes, with differences in timing and density of observed events as well as censoring proportions. event accumulation, providing a strong survival signal that sup- ports exploration of more complex feature combinations. Consid- ering the total number of events, TCGA-KIRC exhibits a slightly more gradual event accumulation, representing an intermediate set- ting in which molecular features may refine clinical predictors. In contrast, TCGA-SARC has fewer observable events, limiting sta- tistical power and increasing uncertainty in multi-omic modeling. These differences provide essential context for interpreting our re- sults, as the ability to identify informative multi-omic feature com- binations can depend directly on the availability of survival signal (number of events) within each cohort. We have benchmarked five setups of the sweeping algorithm in the context of MV MO biomarker discovery for cancer survival pre- diction (Table S1). As inner SV MO optimizer, we used a recently proposed modification of NSGA3 (Deb and Jain 2014): NSGA3- CHS (Cattelani and Fortino 2024), with Cox proportional hazards model as inner survival model (scikit-survival Python module im- plementation). We used 5-fold CV to compare the considered MV strategies and view sets. The MO algorithm aims to find all the best trade-offs of two objectives: the C-index for the survival pre- dictive ability, and the root-leanness as a measure of parsimony in the number of features (Cattelani and Fortino 2024). 2 Supplementary results Figure S4: Expected versus measured C-index comparison of clinical-only and multi-omic feature selection strategies in TCGA-SARC. Top pan- els show C-index as a function of the number of selected features. “Expected” points represent internal cross-validation performance, while “measured” points denote testing performance on the left- out sets. The results from all the 5 folds are plotted together. Left column corresponds to clinical-only optimization, and right col- umn to the multi-omic sweeping strategy integrating clinical with expression-based features. Bottom panels display the distribution of selected feature counts required to achieve increasing concor- dance levels, illustrating model complexity and composition. They show the counts and the expected C-index resulting from optimiz- ing on the whole dataset. Supplementary Fig. S4 shows the comparison of the baseline with respect to the best Sweeping* configuration according to the CHV metric in TCGA-SARC. The best CHV in this setting is obtained by the concatenated configuration with clinic and mRNA views. Anal- ogous comparisons for TCGA-KIRC and TCGA-LGG are in Fig. 3. The most frequently selected features for each Sweeping* con- 5 Figure S5: Top features identified for the TCGA-KIRC cohort across all Sweeping* configurations and view combinations. The figure lists, for each multi-view (clinic; clinic + miRNA; clinic + miRNA + mRNA) and Sweeping* configuration (concatenated; Sw; SwT; CSw; LCSw), the 6 features most fre- quently selected in the approximated Pareto front after optimizing on the whole dataset. The more frequent the feature the darker the background. Figure S6: Top features identified for the TCGA-LGG cohort across all Sweeping* configurations and view combinations. The figure lists, for each multi-view (clinic; clinic + miRNA; clinic + miRNA + mRNA) and Sweeping* configuration (concatenated; Sw; SwT; CSw; LCSw), the 6 features most fre- quently selected in the approximated Pareto front after optimizing on the whole dataset. The more frequent the feature the darker the background. Figure S7: Top features identified for the TCGA-SARC cohort across all Sweeping* configurations and view combinations. The figure lists, for each multi-view (clinic; clinic + miRNA; clinic + miRNA + mRNA) and Sweeping* configuration (concatenated; Sw; SwT; CSw; LCSw), the 6 features most fre- quently selected in the approximated Pareto front after optimizing on the whole dataset. The more frequent the feature the darker the background. 6 figuration, after optimizing on the whole dataset, are reported for TCGA-KIRC (Supplementary Fig. S5), TCGA-LGG (Supplemen- tary Fig. S6), and TCGA-SARC (Supplementary Fig. S7). References L. Cattelani and V. Fortino. Dual-stage optimizer for systematic over- estimation adjustment applied to multi-objective genetic algorithms for biomarker selection. Briefings in Bioinformatics, 26(1):bbae674, 12 2024. ISSN 1477-4054. doi:10.1093/bib/bbae674. K. Deb and H. Jain. An evolutionary many-objective optimiza- tion algorithm using reference-point-based nondominated sorting ap- proach, part i: Solving problems with box constraints. IEEE Transactions on Evolutionary Computation, 18(4):577–601, 2014. doi:10.1109/TEVC.2013.2281535. C. Hutter and J. C. Zenklusen. The cancer genome atlas: cre- ating lasting value beyond its data. Cell, 173(2):283–285, 2018. doi:10.1016/j.cell.2018.03.042. D. Wissel, D. Rowson, and V. Boeva. Systematic comparison of multi-omics survival models reveals a widespread lack of noise re- sistance. Cell Reports Methods, 3(4), 2023. ISSN 2667-2375. doi:10.1016/j.crmeth.2023.100461. 7', 21);
INSERT INTO public.paper_text (paper_text_id, raw_text, cleaned_text, paper_id) VALUES (20, 'LARGE FRINGE TREES FOR RANDOM TREES WITH GIVEN VERTEX
DEGREES
GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON
Abstract. This paper extends the study of fringe trees in random plane trees with a given
degree statistic. While previous work established the asymptotic normality of the count of fringe
trees isomorphic to a fixed tree, we investigate the case where the target tree grows with the
size of the random tree.
We consider three primary subtree counts: the number of fringe trees isomorphic to a specific
growing tree, the number of fringe trees sharing a given growing degree statistic, and the number
of fringe trees of a specific growing size. To establish our results, we employ and compare four
distinct probabilistic frameworks: the method of moments with the Gao–Wormald theorem,
Stein’s method with coupling (to provide explicit error bounds in total variation distance),
the Cai–Devroye method, and Stein’s method with exchangeable pairs. Our findings provide
conditions for Poisson and normal convergence for these subtree counts.
Additionally, we provide a local limit theorem for sums of values obtained via sampling
without replacement that may be of independent interest. Finally, our results and methods are
also applied to conditioned critical Galton–Watson trees.
1. Introduction
In [5], we investigated the fringe trees of random plane trees Tn with a given degree statistic
n (see Section 2 for notation). Specifically, for a sequence of degree statistics pnκqκě1 with size
|nκ| Ñ 8, we established, under suitable conditions, the asymptotic normality of the number of
fringe trees in Tnκ equal (i.e. isomorphic) to a fixed tree T. In the present paper, we extend this
analysis to the number NTκpTnκq of fringe trees of Tnκ that are isomorphic to some given tree
Tκ that grows with κ. More generally, we also consider the number of fringe trees that belong to
the set of trees with given degree statistic mκ, which we denote by NmκpTnκq, and the number
of fringe trees of a given size mκ, denoted by NmκpTnκq.
Our main results are structured as follows. In Section 3, we study NTκpTnκq. We do this
using four different methods, in separate subsections, but the main results are obtained in the
first two subsections. We use several different methods both because the results to some extent
complement each other, and because we find it interesting to show how different methods can be
applied to this problem, and to compare the results. The first subsection derives results by ex-
plicitly computing the factorial moments of NTκpTnκq and analysing their asymptotic behaviour.
In particular, we establish asymptotic normality by applying the Gao—Wormald theorem [14,
Theorem 1] (see also [5, Theorem A.1]). This leads to Theorems 3.2 and 3.3 which provide
conditions for NTκpTnκq to be asymptotically Poisson or normal. In Section 3.2 we use instead
Stein’s method with coupling [2] (with some calculations of first and second moments here too).
This yields a very similar result with Poisson or normal convergence in Corollary 3.11. This
overlaps to a large extent with the result in Theorem 3.3, but the results in the two sections
also complement each other; Theorem 3.3 includes results on moment convergence, while Stein’s
method yields Theorem 3.10 with an explicit estimate of the error (in total variation distance)
