For our purposes, the method by Cai and Devroye [8] thus yields a simpler proof of part of our
main results, but there are exceptional cases (including the case when Tκ “ T is fixed) where it
does not yield convergence in distribution. Moreover, the bound (3.84) for the total variation
distance is inferior to the bound (3.41) obtained by Stein’s method. Nevertheless, we include
the method here since it is simple, and it might be useful in other situations too, and we find it
interesting to compare the result of it with the results from other methods.
3.4. Stein’s method with exchangeable pairs. There are many versions of Stein’s method,
both for Poisson approximation and normal approximation, see e.g. [10] and [30].
We have
in Section 3.2 used Stein’s method with couplings. Other popular versions use exchangeable
pairs. Cai and Devroye used, in a preliminary version [7] of the paper [8] used above, a version
of Stein’s method with exchangeable pairs for Poisson approximation, which was developed by
Chatterjee, Diaconis, and Meckes [9] and further discussed in Ross [30, Section 4.4]. Recall that
a pair pX, X1q of random variables is called an exchangeable pair if pX, X1q
d“ pX1, Xq. We
sketch one way to construct an exchangeable pair in our setting.
Let X :“ NT pTnq, where T is a given tree and n is a given degree sequence. Let V be a
uniformly random vertex of Tn, and let T 1 be a uniformly random tree in TnT , independent of
Tn and V . If the fringe tree pTnqV has the same degree statistic nT as T, then define T 1
n as the
tree Tn with this fringe tree pTnqV replaced by T 1; otherwise, let T 1
n “ Tn. Note that T 1
n has the
same degree statistic n as Tn. Moreover, it is easy to see that pTn, T 1
nq is an exchangeable pair
of random trees. Hence, pNT pTnq, NT pT 1
nqq is an exchangeable pair of random variables.
