6. Application to Galton–Watson Trees
As in our previous paper [5] for the number of fringe trees equal to a fixed tree T (not
depending on κ), we can use results for random trees with given vertex degrees to obtain
corresponding results also for conditioned Galton–Watson trees (or the somewhat more general


LARGE FRINGE TREES
25
simply generated trees) by conditioning on the degree statistic of the tree. For the results in the
present paper, where we consider fringe trees Tκ growing with κ, such results for Galton–Watson
trees have been shown by Cai and Devroye [8] by other methods. (See also [23] for the case of a
fixed tree Tκ “ T.) We will here illustrate the conditioning method by giving alternative proofs
of two of their results, in one case extending it to include also some offspring distributions with
infinite variance.
We consider for simplicity here a conditioned Galton–Watson tree Tp,n obtained from a
Galton–Watson tree Tp with offspring distribution p having mean 1 by conditioning on the
size |Tp| “ n; it is well known, using tiltings, that the results immediately extend to conditioned
Galton–Watson trees with a large class of offspring distributions, and even further to a large
class of simply generated trees, see e.g. [22].
The key fact is (as in the related arguments in [5]) that for any fixed degree statistic n “
pnpiqqiě0 with PpnTp,n “ nq ą 0, it follows from (2.23), see e.g. [1, Proposition 8], that
conditionally given nTp,n “ n, we have Tp,n P UnifpTnq.
