e pkpdκq Ñ pk, where p :“ ppkqkPZ is a probability distribution on Z. (iii) Qκ “ Opnκq. In other words, if Dκ is a random element of the sequence dκ, then Var Dκ “ Op1q. We can now state our local limit theorem. Theorem A.3. Let dκ (κ ě 1) be (finite) integer sequences such that, with notations as above, Condition A.2 holds with limit p nonlattice, and also the Lindeberg condition (A.7) holds. Then, as κ Ñ 8, uniformly for all k P Z, PpSmκ “ kq “ 1 a 2πpσ2κ ˆ exp ˆ ´pk ´ mκ ¯dκq2 2pσ2κ ˙ ` op1q ˙ . (A.11) 30 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON The proof uses the standard method of Fourier inversion using a global limit theorem (Theo- rem A.1) combined with an estimate of the characteristic function away from 0 (and multiples of 2π). Note that both the proof of Theorem A.1 in [12] and the Berry–Esseen estimates in [19; 20] are based on estimates of the characteristic function, but they need only estimates for close to 0 (on the other hand, they need more precise estimates there). Note also that the assumption that p is nonlattice is necessary, since (A.11) cannot hold without modification if, for example, all diκ are even. We prove first a lemma with the required estimate of the characteristic function φSmκptq of Smκ. Lemma A.4. Suppose that Condition A.2(ii) holds, and that p is nonlattice. Then there exist constants C ą 0 and c ą 0 such that, for all κ and all mκ with 0 ď mκ ď nκ, |φSmκptq| ď Ce´cmκp1´mκ{nκqt2, |t| ď π. (A.12) Proof. For convenience, we drop the subscripts κ. We use the conditioning method implicit in [12] (see also [17] and [21, Example 2.3]). Let ξi (1 ď i ď n) be i.i.d. Bernoulli random variables with Ppξi “ 1q “ p :“ m{n, (A.13) let ηi :“ diξi, and define the random vectors ζi :“ pξi, ηiq “ pξi, diξiq. Let Z “ pX, Y q :“ nÿ i“1 ζi. (A.14) Note that X “ řn i“1 ξi has a binomial distribution Bipn, pq. Moreover, it follows from the construction that Sm d“ pY | X “ mq. (A.15) Let φZps, tq :“ E eisX`itY be the characteristic function of Z (for s, t P R). Then it follows from (A.15) that the characteristic function of Sm is, see [12] and [21], φSmptq “ EreitY 1tX “ mus PpX “ mq “ 1 2π PpX “ mq ż π ´π e´imsφZps, tq ds. (A.16) Since (A.14) is a sum of independent variables, we have φZps, tq “ n ź i“1 E eipsξi`tηiq “ n ź i“1 p1 ´ p ` peips`tdiqq “ ź dPZ p1 ´ p ` peips`dtqqndpdq. (A.17) For any u P R, 1 ´ p ` peiu 2 “ p1 ´ pq2 ` p2 ` 2pp1 ´ pq Re eiu “ 1 ´ 2pp1 ´ pqp1 ´ cos uq ď exp ` ´2pp1 ´ pqp1 ´ cos uq ˘ . (A.18) Consequently, (A.17) yields |φZps, tq| ď exp ´ ´pp1 ´ pq ÿ dPZ ndpdq ` 1 ´ cosps ` tdq ˘¯ . (A.19) By assumption, the limit distribution p is nonlattice, and thus the set ti ´ j : i, j P suppppqu is not contained in any proper subgroup of Z; this means that the set generates Z, and thus there exist finite sequences pikqK k“1 and pjkqK k“1 in suppppq and integers νk such that 1 “ K ÿ k“1 νkpik ´ jkq. (A.20) LARGE FRINGE TREES 31 Let D0 :“ tik, jk : 1 ď k ď Ku Ď suppppq. Since D0 is finite, we have for all sufficiently large κ, by (A.10) and Condition A.2, ndpdq n “ ndpdq |d| ě 1 2pd, d P D0. (A.21) We consider in the sequel only such κ, and note that this suffices, since for any given c, (A.12) trivially holds for any fixed κ if we choose C large enough. Then, (A.19) implies, since 1 ´ cosps ` tdq ě 0 for all s, t P R and d P Z, |φZps, tq| ď exp ´ ´1 2pp1 ´ pqn ÿ dPD0 pd ` 1 ´ cosps ` tdq ˘¯ . (A.22) It remains to study the function gps, tq :“ ÿ dPD0 pd ` 1 ´ cosps ` tdq ˘ . (A.23) First, for some c1 ą 0, if |s|, |t| ă c1, then cosps ` tdq ď 1 ´ 1 3ps ` tdq2 for d P D0 and thus gps, tq ě hps, tq :“ 1 3 ÿ dPD0 pdps ` tdq2. (A.24) The right-hand side hps, tq is a homogeneous polynomial in s and t. Assume hps, tq “ 0. Then, s`td “ 0 for every d P D0. In particular, s`tik “ s`tjk “ 0 for every ik and jk in (A.20); hence, tpik ´ jkq “ 0, and (A.20) implies t “ řK k“1 νkpik ´ jkqt “ 0. Hence also s “ 0. Consequently, hps, tq “ 0 ðñ ps, tq “ p0, 0q. By compactness, hps, tq has a positiv