', 'LARGE FRINGE TREES FOR RANDOM TREES WITH GIVEN VERTEX DEGREES GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON Abstract. This paper extends the study of fringe trees in random plane trees with a given degree statistic. While previous work established the asymptotic normality of the count of fringe trees isomorphic to a fixed tree, we investigate the case where the target tree grows with the size of the random tree. We consider three primary subtree counts: the number of fringe trees isomorphic to a specific growing tree, the number of fringe trees sharing a given growing degree statistic, and the number of fringe trees of a specific growing size. To establish our results, we employ and compare four distinct probabilistic frameworks: the method of moments with the Gao–Wormald theorem, Stein’s method with coupling (to provide explicit error bounds in total variation distance), the Cai–Devroye method, and Stein’s method with exchangeable pairs. Our findings provide conditions for Poisson and normal convergence for these subtree counts. Additionally, we provide a local limit theorem for sums of values obtained via sampling without replacement that may be of independent interest. Finally, our results and methods are also applied to conditioned critical Galton–Watson trees. 1. Introduction In [5], we investigated the fringe trees of random plane trees Tn with a given degree statistic n (see Section 2 for notation). Specifically, for a sequence of degree statistics pnκqκě1 with size |nκ| Ñ 8, we established, under suitable conditions, the asymptotic normality of the number of fringe trees in Tnκ equal (i.e. isomorphic) to a fixed tree T. In the present paper, we extend this analysis to the number NTκpTnκq of fringe trees of Tnκ that are isomorphic to some given tree Tκ that grows with κ. More generally, we also consider the number of fringe trees that belong to the set of trees with given degree statistic mκ, which we denote by NmκpTnκq, and the number of fringe trees of a given size mκ, denoted by NmκpTnκq. Our main results are structured as follows. In Section 3, we study NTκpTnκq. We do this using four different methods, in separate subsections, but the main results are obtained in the first two subsections. We use several different methods both because the results to some extent complement each other, and because we find it interesting to show how different methods can be applied to this problem, and to compare the results. The first subsection derives results by ex- plicitly computing the factorial moments of NTκpTnκq and analysing their asymptotic behaviour. In particular, we establish asymptotic normality by applying the Gao—Wormald theorem [14, Theorem 1] (see also [5, Theorem A.1]). This leads to Theorems 3.2 and 3.3 which provide conditions for NTκpTnκq to be asymptotically Poisson or normal. In Section 3.2 we use instead Stein’s method with coupling [2] (with some calculations of first and second moments here too). This yields a very similar result with Poisson or normal convergence in Corollary 3.11. This overlaps to a large extent with the result in Theorem 3.3, but the results in the two sections also complement each other; Theorem 3.3 includes results on moment convergence, while Stein’s method yields Theorem 3.10 with an explicit estimate of the error (in total variation distance) Date: 7 April, 2026. Funded by Knut and Alice Wallenberg Foundation; Ragnar S¨oderberg’s Foundation; Swedish Research Council. 1 arXiv:2604.06012v1 [math.PR] 7 Apr 2026 2 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON for the approximations. In Section 3.3, we apply a different, and simpler, method by Cai and Devroye [8]. We show that this method also gives a bound for the error in total variation distance, which in most cases is sufficient to show convergence in distribution to a Poisson or normal distribution; however, this bound is weaker than the bound obtained in Section 3.2 by Stein’s method. Finally, in Section 3.4, we discuss briefly another version of Stein’s method, with exchangeable pairs. It turns out that for the present problem, this gives us the same bound as the simpler method in Section 3.3; we therefore do not treat this method in detail. In Section 4, we study NmκpTnκq, the number of fringe trees with a given degree statistics but otherwise arbitrary shape. Theorem 4.2 gives results on Poisson and normal convergence, corresponding to Theorem 3.3. We prove this using moment calculations as in Section 3.1. It seems straightforward to use Stein’s method as in Section 3.2 to obtain bounds for the approx- imation error in total variation here too, but we leave that to the readers. (The methods of Sections 3.3 and 3.4 are not applicable here, since they are based on the existence of different fringe trees with the same degree statistics, of which some are counted and some not.) Section 5 then focuses on the asymptotic behaviour of the size-specific count NmκpTnκq. Specifically, Theorem 5.2 establishes that NmκpTnκq converges to a Poisson random variable whenever mκ „ a|nκ|2{3 as κ Ñ 8, for some constant a ą 0. The proof uses a local limit theorem for sums of values obtained via sampling without replacement, Theorem A.3, which may be of independent interest. Finally, in Section 6, we apply our results to conditioned critical Galton–Watson trees. In particular, Theorem 6.1 provides an alternative proof of results in [8, Theorem 1.4 (ii)], while Theorem 6.2 extends [8, Theorem 1.3] to offspring distributions with infinite variance. In Appendix A, we prove a local limit theorem for the sum of a number of values obtained by drawing without replacement that we need in Section 5; this is presumably known, but we have not found a reference and include a proof for completeness. 2. Some background and notation 2.1. Some general notation. Let N0 :“ t0, 1, . . . u and N :“ t1, . . . u. For n P N, let rns :“ t1, . . . , nu. For x P R and q P N0, let pxqq :“ xpx ´ 1q ¨ ¨ ¨ px ´ q ` 1q denote the qth falling factorial of x. (Here pxq0 :“ 1. Note that pxqq “ 0 whenever x P N0 and x ´ q ` 1 ď 0.) We let C and c denote unspecified positive constants that may vary from one occurrence to the next; we may add subscripts to distinguish some of them. We use d ÝÑ for convergence in distribution, for a sequence of random variables in some metric space. Also, d“ means equal in distribution. We write Np0, 1q for a standard normal distribution with mean 0 and variance 1. For λ P r0, 8q, we write Po ` λ ˘ for a Poisson distribution of parameter λ. Let p “ ppiqiPZ be a probability distribution on Z. We define its support suppppq :“ ti P Z : pi ą 0u, and let the span of p be the largest integer h such that i ´ j is a multiple of h for all i, j P suppppq; if p is concentrated at a single point, we define the span to be 8. In symbols, the span is gcdti ´ j : i, j P suppppqu. We will usually consider p with p0 ą 0, and then the span equals gcdpsuppppqq. We say that p is nonlattice if its span equals 1, and lattice otherwise. The total variation distance between two random variables X and Y in a metric space (or rather between their distributions) is defined by dTVpX, Y q :“ sup A PpX P Aq ´ PpY P Aq , (2.1) taking the supremum over all measurable subsets A. LARGE FRINGE TREES 3 Unspecified limits are as κ Ñ 8. 2.2. Trees with given vertex degrees. All trees in this paper are rooted and plane (also called ordered rooted trees); see e.g. [11]. Let T be the set of all (finite) plane rooted trees. Denote the size, i.e. the number of vertices, of a tree T by |T|. Let Tn :“ tT P T : |T| “ nu, for n ě 1. The (out)degree of a vertex v P T, denoted dT pvq, is its number of children in T. The degree statistic of a rooted tree T is the sequence nT “ pnT piqqiě0, where nT piq :“ #tv P T : dT pvq “ iu is the number of vertices of T with i children. We have |T| “ ÿ iě0 nT piq “ 1 ` ÿ iě0 inT piq. (2.2) Moreover, a sequence n “ pnpiqqiě0 of nonnegative integers is the degree statistic of some tree if and only if ÿ iě0 npiq “ 1 ` ÿ iě0 inpiq. (2.3) We say that n is a degree statistic if (2.3) holds. In this case, we let |n| :“ ř iě0 npiq, and we write Tn for the set of plane rooted trees with degree statistic n. It is well known that (see e.g. [28, Exercise 6.2.1]) |Tn| “ 1 |n| ˆ|n| n ˙ “ 1 |n| |n|! ś iě0 npiq!. (2.4) We let Tn be a uniformly random element of the set Tn, and we denote this by Tn P UnifpTnq. 2.3. Degree statistics and empirical degree distributions. For a degree statistic n, denote by ppnq “ ppipnqqiě0 its empirical degree distribution, i.e., pipnq :“ npiq |n| , for i ě 0. (2.5) Note that this is the distribution of the degree of a uniformly random vertex in any tree T with degree statistic n. In this paper (as in [5]), we assume the following condition. Condition 2.1. nκ “ pnκpiqqiě0, κ ě 1, are degree statistics such that as κ Ñ 8: (i) |nκ| Ñ 8, (ii) For every i ě 0, we have pipnκq Ñ pi, where p :“ ppiqiě0 is a probability distribution on N0. Remark 2.2. Let Dκ denote a random variable with the distribution ppnκq; by (2.5), Dκ can be regarded as the degree of a randomly chosen vertex of any tree with degree statistic nκ. Similarly, let Dp be a random variable with distribution p. Then Condition 2.1(ii) says that Dκ d ÝÑ Dp, as κ Ñ 8. Furthermore, (2.3) implies E Dκ “ |nκ| ´ 1 |nκ| “ 1 ´ 1 |nκ|, (2.6) and it follows from (2.6) and Fatou’s lemma that Condition 2.1 implies ÿ iě0 ipi “ E Dp ď 1. (2.7) 4 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON We have equality in (2.7) if and only if the variables Dκ are uniformly integrable (see e.g. [16, Theorem 5.5.9]). △ We note also that, similarly to the asymptotic result (2.7), for any degree statistic n, we have by (2.3) ÿ iě0 ipipnq “ 1 ´ 1 |n| ă 1. (2.8) In particular, it follows that ÿ iě2 pipnq ď 1 2 ÿ iě2 ipipnq ă 1 2, (2.9) and thus p0pnq ` p1pnq ą 1 2. (2.10) In Section 5, but not in Sections 3–4, we will also need the following condition. Condition 2