ly distributed elements of TnT , and consequently, the number of these fringe trees that are equal to T has conditionally a binomial distribution. too, and thus, conditioned on NnT pTnq (defined in (2.13)), we have NT pTnq P Bi ` NnT pTnq, 1{|TnT | ˘ . (3.76) We can thus use the method by Cai and Devroye [8], who showed the following general result. Lemma 3.12 ([8, Lemma 2.10]). Let X and M be non-negative integer-valued random variables. If conditioned on the event M “ m, X is binomial pm, pq, then dTV ` X, PopE Xq ˘ ď p ` c pVar M E M . (3.77) Combining (3.76) and (3.77), we find, letting again λ :“ ErNT pTnqs, dTV ` NT pTnq, Popλq ˘ ď 1 |TnT | ` d VarrNnT pTnqs |TnT | ¨ ErNnT pTnqs. (3.78) Here the mean and variance of NnT pTnq enter. These can easily be expressed using the mean and variance of NT pTnq as follows. Lemma 3.13. Let n be a degree statistic and let Tn P UnifpTnq. For T P T, ENnT pTnq “ |TnT | ¨ ENT pTnq, (3.79) VarpNnT pTnqq “ |TnT |2 ¨ VarpNT pTnqq ´ |TnT | ¨ p|TnT | ´ 1q ¨ ENT pTnq “ |TnT |2 ¨ ` Var NT pTnq ´ E NT pTnq ˘ ` |TnT | ¨ E NT pTnq. (3.80) Proof. [5, Lemma 3.1] shows that E NT 1pTnq depends only on n and the degree statistic of T, and thus, for any T 1 P TnT , E NT 1pTnq “ E NT pTnq. Hence, (3.79) follows from (2.13). Furthermore, (2.13) yields also pNnT pTnqq2 “ ÿ T 1PTnT pNT 1pTnqq2 ` ÿ T 1PTnT ÿ T 2PTnT NT 1pTnqNT 2pTnq1tT 1‰T 2u. (3.81) If T 1, T 2 P TnT such that T 1 ‰ T 2, then two different fringe trees in Tn that are isomorphic to T 1 have to be disjoint, and the same holds for two fringe trees isomorphic to T 1 and T 2, respectively. It follows easily by the arguments in the proof of [5, Lemma 3.1], see also [5, Lemmas 3.2 and 3.3] for more general and more detailed results, that ErpNT 1pTnqq2s “ ErpNT pTnqq2s and ErNT 1pTnqNT 2pTnqs “ ErpNT pTnqq2s. Hence, it follows from (3.81) that E “ NnT pTnq2‰ “ |TnT |E “ NT pTnq2‰ ` |TnT | ¨ p|TnT | ´ 1q ¨ ` E “ NT pTnq2‰ ´ E “ NT pTnq ‰˘ , (3.82) and (3.80) follows. □ LARGE FRINGE TREES 17 Using (3.78) and Lemma 3.13, we obtain dTV ` NT pTnq, Popλq ˘ ď 1 |TnT | ` d VarrNT pTnqs ´ E NT pTnq ErNT pTnqs ` 1 |TnT | ď d VarrNT pTnqs ´ E NT pTnq ErNT pTnqs ` 2 a |TnT |. (3.83) Using (3.70) from the proof above, we obtain from (3.83) dTV ` NT pTnq, Popλq ˘ “ O ` ∆1{2 ` |TnT |´1{2˘ . (3.84) (The proof of (3.70) assumes ∆ă 1 4λ, but (3.84) holds also for ∆ě 1 4λ by (3.43).) This is weaker than (3.41), but enough to prove Corollary 3.11 provided |TnTκ| Ñ 8, which can be shown to hold provided |Tκ| Ñ 8 except for the two cases when Tκ is a path or a star. (In these exceptional cases, |TnTκ| “ 1, and the method is useless.) For our purposes, the method by Cai and Devroye [8] thus yields a simpler proof of part of our main results, but there are exceptional cases (including the case when Tκ “ T is fixed) where it does not yield convergence in distribution. Moreover, the bound (3.84) for the total variation distance is inferior to the bound (3.41) obtained by Stein’s method. Nevertheless, we include the method here since it is simple, and it might be useful in other situations too, and we find it interesting to compare the result of it with the results from other methods. 3.4. Stein’s method with exchangeable pairs. There are many versions of Stein’s method, both for Poisson approximation and normal approximation, see e.g. [10] and [30]. We have in Section 3.2 used Stein’s method with couplings. Other popular versions use exchangeable pairs. Cai and Devroye used, in a preliminary version [7] of the paper [8] used above, a version of Stein’s method with exchangeable pairs for Poisson approximation, which was developed by Chatterjee, Diaconis, and Meckes [9] and further discussed in Ross [30, Section 4.4]. Recall that a pair pX, X1q of random variables is called an exchangeable pair if pX, X1q d“ pX1, Xq. We sketch one way to construct an exchangeable pair in our setting. Let X :“ NT pTnq, where T is a given tree and n is a given degree sequence. Let V be a uniformly random vertex of Tn, and let T 1 be a uniformly random tree in TnT , independent of Tn and V . If the fringe tree pTnqV has the same degree statistic nT as T, then define T 1 n as the tree Tn with this fringe tree pTnqV replaced by T 1; otherwise, let T 1 n “ Tn. Note that T 1 n has the same degree statistic n as Tn. Moreover, it is easy to see that pTn, T 1 nq is an exchangeable pair of random trees. Hence, pNT pTnq, NT pT 1 nqq is an exchangeable pair of random variables. Using this exchangeable pair and [30, Theorem 4.5 and equation (4.14)], see also [9, Lemma 2 and Proposition 3], it can be shown after lengthy calculations that dTV ` NT pTnq, PopErNT pTnqsq ˘ ď 1 |TnT | ` ˆVarpNT pTnqq ´ ENT pTnq ENT pTnq ` 1 |TnT | ˙1{2 ď 2 |TnT |1{2 ` ˆVarpNT pTnqq ´ ENT pTnq ENT pTnq ˙1{2 . (3.85) Note that this is exactly the same estimate as (3.83), obtained by a much simpler method. We therefore find (as apparently the authors of [8] did for their problem) that in our case, this version of Stein’s method works, but it is not really useful since the simpler method in Section 3.3 yields the same result in a simpler way. We therefore omit the calculations leading to (3.85). (This does not exclude the possibility that better results might be obtained in the future by 18 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON this method using some other exchangeable pair, or the same pair and different estimates in the calculations.) 4. Fringe trees with a given degree statistic (depending on κ) For T P T and a degree statistic n, recall that we have defined NnpTq in (2.13) to be the number of fringe trees of T with degree statistic n. Lemma 4.1. Let n and m be two degree statistics and let Tn P UnifpTnq. Then, for every q P N, we have ErpNmpTnqqqs “ |Tm|q |n| p|n|qq|m|´q`1 ź iě0 pnpiqqqmpiq, (4.1) where we (in the case |n| ă q|m| ´ q ` 1) interpret 0 0 “ 0. Equivalently, for any tree T with degree statistic m, we have ErpNmpTnqqqs “ |Tm|q ErpNT pTnqqqs. (4.2) Proof. Fo