 more than one fringe tree 24 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON of this size when |nκ| ą 2ℓ. We have πℓą 0 for all large ℓ, but the example above shows that πℓ“ 0 is possible for some small ℓ. △ Remark 5.4. We conjecture that when mκ ! |nκ|2{3, and thus ErNmκpTnκqs Ñ 8 by Lemma 5.1, we have asymptotic normality, in analogy with Theorems 3.2, 3.3, and 4.2. It might be possible to prove this using the Gao–Wormald theorem, as in the other theorems just mentioned, but since this would require estimates of moments of unbounded order, the estimates in the proof of Theorem 5.2 are not precise enough; we would need either a better estimate of the error in Theorem A.3, or a different, non-inductive, way to estimate (5.4) or (5.12). △ Remark 5.5. We have assumed in both Lemma 5.1 and Theorem 5.2 that the asymptotic degree distribution p is nonlattice. The results are easily extended to the case when all degrees diκ are divisible by some fixed integer h ą 1, for example if all degrees are even; we may then apply Theorem A.3 to the integers diκ{h. There are, however, also limiting cases. For example, suppose that p is concentrated on the even integers, and thus lattice, but that nκp1q ą 0 although nκp1q “ op|nκ|q. In Example 5.6 below, one example of this type is studied in some detail, and it is seen that in the critical case mκ “ Θp|nκ|2{3q, there will periodicities in the result if nκp1q “ Op|nκ|1{3q, but it seems that these disappear if nκp1q is larger. We believe that this behaviour is typical for cases with p lattice, but we have not pursued this further, and leave such cases to the reader. △ Example 5.6. Suppose that |nκ| Ñ 8 with nκpiq ą 0 only for i P t0, 1, 2u, and that nκp1q „ b|nκ|1{3 for some constant b ą 0. Then it follows from (2.3) that necessarily nκp0q “ 1 2p|nκ| ´ nκp1q ` 1q “ nκp2q ` 1, and thus Condition 2.1 holds with p0 “ p2 “ 1 2; hence p is concentrated on t0, 2u and thus has span 2. Condition 2.3 holds too. Let mκ „ a|nκ|2{3 for some a ą 0, let d be uniformly random in Bnκ, and let Yκ :“ |ti ď mκ : diκ “ 1u|. It is easy to see that as κ Ñ 8, Yκ d ÝÑ Y P Popabq. Conditioned on Yκ, the remaining mκ ´ Yκ degrees diκ with i ď mκ are all 0 or 2, so their sum is even, and the number of 2s has a hypergeometric distribution; it follows easily from this (for example using Theorem A.3) that for every fixed y P N0, PrSmκ “ mκ ´ 1 | Yκ “ ys “ # 0, y ı mκ ´ 1 pmod 2q, 2 ?2πmκ p1 ` op1qq, y ” mκ ´ 1 pmod 2q. (5.20) Furthermore, this holds uniformly for all y ď m1{2 κ , say, and it follows easily from (5.3) that, cf. (5.10) and Lemma 5.1(ii) in the nonlattice case, E NmκpTnκq “ |nκ| mκ PrSmκ “ mκ ´ 1 | Yκ “ ys „ 2|nκ| ? 2πmκ3 P ` Yκ ” mκ ´ 1 pmod 2q ˘ „ #1`e´ab ? 2πa3 , mκ is odd, 1´e´ab ? 2πa3 , mκ is even. (5.21) Higher factorial moments can be calculated asymptotically by the method in the proof of The- orem 5.2, and it follows that NmκpTnκq has one asymptotic Poisson distribution for even mκ, and a different asymptotic Poisson distribution for odd mκ. □ 6. Application to Galton–Watson Trees As in our previous paper [5] for the number of fringe trees equal to a fixed tree T (not depending on κ), we can use results for random trees with given vertex degrees to obtain corresponding results also for conditioned Galton–Watson trees (or the somewhat more general LARGE FRINGE TREES 25 simply generated trees) by conditioning on the degree statistic of the tree. For the results in the present paper, where we consider fringe trees Tκ growing with κ, such results for Galton–Watson trees have been shown by Cai and Devroye [8] by other methods. (See also [23] for the case of a fixed tree Tκ “ T.) We will here illustrate the conditioning method by giving alternative proofs of two of their results, in one case extending it to include also some offspring distributions with infinite variance. We consider for simplicity here a conditioned Galton–Watson tree Tp,n obtained from a Galton–Watson tree Tp with offspring distribution p having mean 1 by conditioning on the size |Tp| “ n; it is well known, using tiltings, that the results immediately extend to conditioned Galton–Watson trees with a large class of offspring distributions, and even further to a large class of simply generated trees, see e.g. [22]. The key fact is (as in the related arguments in [5]) that for any fixed degree statistic n “ pnpiqqiě0 with PpnTp,n “ nq ą 0, it follows from (2.23), see e.g. [1, Proposition 8], that conditionally given nTp,n “ n, we have Tp,n P UnifpTnq. (6.1) We begin with a straightforward conditioning argument applied to the number of fringe trees of a given size. The result is essentially a special case of [8, Theorem 1.4(ii)] (there proved directly by the method of moments), although we have also computed the (asymptotic) expectation. Theorem 6.1 ([8, Theorem 1.4(ii)]). Let p :“ ppiqiě0 be a probability distribution on N0 such that p0 ą 0 and pi ą 0 for at least one i ě 2. Let Tp,n be a Galton–Watson tree with offspring distribution p conditioned to have size n P N (we only consider n along values for which the conditioning is well defined). Furthermore, suppose that p is nonlattice, the mean ř iě0 ipi “ 1, and the variance σ2 p :“ ř iě0pi ´ 1q2pi ă 8. Let mn be integers with 1 ď mn ď n such that mn „ an2{3 as n Ñ 8, for some constant a ą 0. Then, as n Ñ 8, NmnpTp,nq d ÝÑ Po ` p2πσ2 pa3q´1{2˘ . (6.2) Proof. Let PpN0q be the set of probability measures on N0 equipped with the weak topology. By [6, Lemma 11], we have that, as n Ñ 8, ´ ppnTp,nq, σ2 ppnTp,nq ¯ d ÝÑ pp, σ2 pq, (6.3) where the convergence holds in the space PpN0qˆR equipped with the product topology. By the Skorohod coupling theorem [26, Theorem 4.30], we can and will assume that the convergence in (6.3) holds a.s.; in other words, Condition 2.1 and Condition 2.3 hold a.s. for the degree statistics nTp,n, with p. Moreover, e.g. by resampling Tp,n conditioned on nTp,n, we may assume that conditioned on the sequence of degree statistics pnTp,nq8 n“1, the random trees Tp,n, n ě 1, are independent. As noted above, for any fixed degree statistic n with PpnTp,n “ nq ą 0, we have (6.1). It follows that we may apply Theorem 5.2 conditioned on the sequence of degree statistics pnTp,nq8 n“1; this shows that (6.2) holds conditioned on pnTp,nq8 n“1. Then, (6.2) also holds unconditionally by the dominated convergence theorem. □ The same argument can be used to give versions for the conditioned Galton–Watson tree Tp,n of the Poisson convergence parts of Theorems 3.2 and 4.2. In principle, it should be possible to apply this method also to the parts with normal convergence, but then we would have to also take into account the random fluctuations of the degree statistic of the conditioned Galton– Watson tree Tp,n, see [5, Section 7] where this is done in detail for NT pTp,nq where T is a fixed tree. Instead we go back to the proofs above and use moment calculations there to show the 26 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON following result which includes [8, Theorem 1.3] (although without an explicit error bound) and extends it to some cases where p has infinite variance. Theorem 6.2 (Partly [8, Theorem 1.3]). Let p :“ ppiqiě0 be a probability distribution on N0 such that p0 ą 0 and pi ą 0 for at least one i ě 2. Let Tp,n be a Galton–Watson tree with offspring distribution p conditioned to have size n P N (we only consider n along values for which the conditioning is well defined). Furthermore, suppose that the mean ř iě0 ipi “ 1 and that p satisfies one of the following conditions: (a) σ2 p :“ ř iě0pi ´ 1q2pi P p0, 8q. (I.e., the distribution p has finite variance σ2.) (b) σ2 p “ 8 and p belongs to the domain of attraction of a stable law of index α P p1, 2s. (The last condition is equivalent to that there exists a slowly varying function L : R` Ñ R` such that řk i“0 i2pi “ k2´αLpkq, as k Ñ 8 [13, Theorem XVII.5.2].) Let Tn P T be such that mn :“ |Tn| Ñ 8. As n Ñ 8, (i) If nπppTnq Ñ λ, for some λ P r0, 8q, then NTnpTp,nq d ÝÑ Po ` λ ˘ (6.4) with convergence of all moments. (ii) If nπppTnq Ñ 8, then NTnpTp,nq ´ nπppTnq a nπppTnq d ÝÑ N ` 0, 1 ˘ . (6.5) Proof. We use again (6.1). Hence, it follows from [5, Lemma 3.3(i)], see (3.27), that, for q P N such that n ě qmn ´ q ` 1, EpNTnpTp,nqqq “ n pnqqmn´q`1 E ” ź iě0 pnTp,npiqqqnTnpiq ı . (6.6) Let ξ1, ξ2, . . . , be a sequence of independent random variables with distribution p, and define the partial sums Sn “ řn i“1 ξi, n ě 0. It follows from (6.6) together with the bijection Υ : Tn Ø Dn and the n-to-1 map Φ : Bn Ñ Dn discussed in Section 2.5 that EpNTnpTp,nqqq “ n ź iě0 pqnTnpiq i ¨ pnqqmn pnqqmn´q`1 PpSn´qmn “ n ´ 1 ´ qpmn ´ 1qq PpSn “ n ´ 1q ; (6.7) see e.g. [5, (B.4)] (with qin “ qnTnpiq, for i ě 0). We first prove (i) for λ ą 0 and (ii). Since then nπppTnq Ñ λ, as n Ñ 8, for some λ P p0, 8s, we may consider n large enough such that πppTnq ą 0. Let qn P N be such that qn “ OppnπppTnqq1{2q. (6.8) Note that then q2 nm2 n n “ q2 nm2 nπppTnq nπppTnq “ O ´ m2 nπppTnq ¯ “ O ´ m2 n sup iě0 pmn i ¯ “ op1q. (6.9) This allows us to consider only n large enough such that n ě qnmn ´ qn ` 1. Then, by (6.7), (3.4), and (6.9), EpNTnpTp,nqqqn “ pnπppTnqqqn PpSn´qnmn “ n ´ 1 ´ qnpmn ´ 1qq PpSn “ n ´ 1q ¨ exppop1qq. (6.10) Next, we use a suitable local limit theorem for the sums pSn, n ě 0q. We consider the cases (a) and (b) separately. LARGE FRINGE TREES 27 (a): In this case, the distribution p “ ppiqiě0 has mean 1 and finite variance σ2 p ą 0. Let h ě 1 be the span of this distribution, i.e., the largest integer such that ξ1 a.s. is a multiple of h. The standard local central limit theorem, see e.g. [27, Theorem 7.1], yields, recalling (6.9), PpSn´qnmn “ n ´ 1 ´ qnpmn ´ 1qq “ h b 2πσ2pn exp ´ ´ pqn ´ 1q2 2σ2ppn ´ qnmnq ` op1q ¯ “ h b 2πσ2pn exppop1qq, (6.11) PpSn “ n ´ 1q “ h b 2πσ2pn exppop1qq. (6.12) Hence, (6.10) yields, EpNTnpTp,nqqqn “ pnπppTnqqqn ¨ exppop1qq. (6.13) In case (i), the condition (6.8) says q “ Op1q. Hence, for every fixed q P N, (6.13) applies and yields EpNTnpTp,nqqq „ pnπppTnqqq, (6.14) as n Ñ 8; consequently, (6.4) follows by the methods of moments. Note also that (6.14) implies the convergence of all moments of NTnpTp,nq as claimed in (i). In case (ii), it follows from (6.13) that, for qn “ OppnπppTnqq1{2q, EpNTnpTp,nqqqn “ pnπppTnqqqn ¨ exp ´nπppTnq ´ nπppTnq 2n2πppTnq2 q2 n ` op1q ¯ . (6.15) Hence, (6.5) follows by applying the Gao–Wormald theorem [14, Theorem 1] (or [5, Theorem A.1] with m “ 1) with µn “ σ2 n “ nπppTnq. (b): This is similar. By assumption, there exist sequences of constants an ą 0 and bn such that pSn ´bnq{an converges in distribution to some stable random variable Y of index α P p1, 2s; since α ą 1, we may here choose bn “ E Sn “ n [13, Theorem XVII.5.3]. Moreover, it follows from [13, XVII.(5.24)] that the sequence an is regularly varying with exponent 1{α, and in particular that that if n1pnq “ n ` opnq, then an1pnq „ an. Let gY pxq be the density function of the stable limit Y , and note that gY pxq is continuous and that gY pxq ą 0 for every x P R (see e.g. [31, Remark 4 after Theorem 2.2.3, pp. 79–80]). Since we assume that the variance of ξ1 is infinite, we have an " n1{2 (see [13, XVII.(5.23)]). Let again h be the span of the distribution p, and note that Tp,n exists only if n ” 1 pmod hq, and that πppTnq ą 0 implies mn “ |Tn| ” 1 pmod hq; we may thus assume these congruences. Then, by recalling (6.9), the local limit theorem (see [15, § 50]) yields, with n1pnq :“ n´qnmn “ n´opnq, PpSn´qnmn “ n ´ 1 ´ qnpmn ´ 1qq “ h an1pnq ´ gY ´qn ´ 1 an1pnq ¯ ` op1q ¯ “ h an gY p0q exppop1qq, (6.16) PpSn “ n ´ 1q “ h an gY p0q exppop1qq. (6.17) Hence, (6.10) yields (6.13) in this case too. Then, the proof of (i) and (ii) in case (b) is completed as above. Finally, we prove the case λ “ 0 of (i). If mn “ op?nq, then (6.9) holds for qn “ q fixed, and thus (6.14) holds by the proof above, which yields the conclusion. 28 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON It remains to consider large mn, say mn ě n1{3. We consider again only n ” 1 pmod hq, since otherwise Tp,n does not exist. By the local limit theorem (6.12) or (6.17), it follows that (with an “ n1{2 in case (a)), for some c ą 0, PpSn “ n ´ 1q „ c an . (6.18) Since an is regularly varying with exponent 1{α ă 1, (6.18) implies that for large n, PpSn “ n ´ 1q ą n´1. (6.19) It follows from (6.7) (with q “ 1), together with (6.19), that E NTnpTp,nq “ nπppTnqPpSn´mn “ n ´ mnq PpSn “ n ´ 1q ď n2πppTnq ď n2pmax i piqmn Ñ 0, (6.20) since maxi pi ă 1 and we assumed mn ě n1{3. We immediately obtain also convergence of higher moments: E “ NTnpTp,nqq‰ ď nq´1 E “ NTnpTp,nq ‰ ď nq`1pmax i piqmn ď nq`1pmax i piqn1{3 Ñ 0. (6.21) □ We can from Theorem 4.2 obtain similar results for NmκpTp,nq, the number of fringe trees with a given degree statistic mκ, but we leave the details to the reader. Appendix A. A local limit theorem for drawing without replacement We used above a local limit theorem for the sum of a number of values obtained by drawing without replacement. We consider here the situation in somewhat greater generality. Let d “ pd1, . . . , dnq be a given sequence of real numbers, and let Sm (0 ď m ď n) be the sum of m of them, obtained by drawing without replacement. Formally, we may take a uniformly random permutation τ of t1, . . . , nu and define Sm “ Smpdq :“ m ÿ i“1 dτpiq. (A.1) It is well known that for large m and n ´ m, under suitable conditions on d, the distribution of Sm is asymptotically normal. To state the result formally, we suppose that we are given a sequence dκ “ pdiκqnκ i“1, κ ě 1, of sequences of real numbers, where pnκqκě1 is some sequence of natural numbers; we also suppose that pmκqκě1 is another given sequence with 0 ď mκ ď nκ for every κ ě 1, and we consider the random sums Smκ “ Smκpdκq. (We simplify the notation, since there is no risk of confusion.) We define ¯dκ :“ 1 nκ nk ÿ i“1 diκ, (A.2) Qκ :“ nk ÿ i“1 pdiκ ´ ¯dκq2, (A.3) pσ2 κ :“ mκ nκ ˆ 1 ´ mκ nκ ˙ Qκ. (A.4) It is easily seen that E Smκ “ mκ ¯dκ and Var Smκ “ mκpnκ ´ mκq nκpnκ ´ 1q Qκ “ nκ nκ ´ 1pσ2 κ. (A.5) LARGE FRINGE TREES 29 (We use pσ2 κ as a convenient approximation to Var Smκ; in the asymptotic results below it does not matter which one we use.) Note also that if Dκ is a random element of the sequence dκ, then E Dκ “ ¯dκ and Var Dκ “ Qκ{nκ. (A.6) (This is the case mκ “ 1 of (A.5).) To avoid trivialities, we assume Qκ ą 0; otherwise, diκ “ ¯dκ for all i, and Smκ is non-random. We assume the following Lindeberg type condition: 1 Qκ ÿ |diκ´ ¯dκ|ąεpσκ pdiκ ´ ¯dκq2 Ñ 0 as κ Ñ 8, for every ε ą 0. (A.7) Then the following central limit theorem was proved by Erd˝os and R´enyi [12], see also H´ajek [17] for another (simpler) proof (and proof of necessity of (A.7)), and H´ajek [18] and Jiˇrina [25] for generalizations. Theorem A.1 ([12]). With notations as above, if (A.7) holds, then, as κ Ñ 8, Smκ ´ E Smκ pσκ “ Smκ ´ mκ ¯dκ pσκ d ÝÑ Np0, 1q. (A.8) Further known results are a functional limit theorem [29] and Berry–Esseen estimates on the rate of convergence [19; 20]. Our purpose is to show a corresponding local limit theorem, in the case when all di are integers. We guess that also such a local limit theorem has been proved earlier, but we have failed to find any reference except [3], which we have been unable to obtain, and we therefore give a rather general theorem with a detailed proof. Given a sequence d “ pd1, . . . , dnq of integers, we define |d| :“ n and, in analogy with Section 2.2, the statistic nd “ pndpiqqiPZ, where ndpkq :“ #t1 ď i ď |d| : di “ ku. (A.9) We define also pkpdq :“ ndpkq |d| , k P Z; (A.10) thus ppkpdqqkPZ is a probability distribution, namely, the empirical distribution of pdiqn i“1. We assume a condition similar to (and slightly weaker than) Conditions 2.1 and 2.3: Condition A.2. dκ “ pdiκqnκ i“1, κ ě 1, are sequences such that, as κ Ñ 8: (i) |dκ| Ñ 8, (ii) For every k P Z, we have pkpdκq Ñ pk, where p :“ ppkqkPZ is a probability distribution on Z. (iii) Qκ “ Opnκq. In other words, if Dκ is a random element of the sequence dκ, then Var Dκ “ Op1q. We can now state our local limit theorem. Theorem A.3. Let dκ (κ ě 1) be (finite) integer sequences such that, with notations as above, Condition A.2 holds with limit p nonlattice, and also the Lindeberg condition (A.7) holds. Then, as κ Ñ 8, uniformly for all k P Z, PpSmκ “ kq “ 1 a 2πpσ2κ ˆ exp ˆ ´pk ´ mκ ¯dκq2 2pσ2κ ˙ ` op1q ˙ . (A.11) 30 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON The proof uses the standard method of Fourier inversion using a global limit theorem (Theo- rem A.1) combined with an estimate of the characteristic function away from 0 (and multiples of 2π). Note that both the proof of Theorem A.1 in [12] and the Berry–Esseen estimates in [19; 20] are based on estimates of the characteristic function, but they need only estimates for close to 0 (on the other hand, they need more precise estimates there). Note also that the assumption that p is nonlattice is necessary, since (A.11) cannot hold without modification if, for example, all diκ are even. We prove first a lemma with the required estimate of the characteristic function φSmκptq of Smκ. Lemma A.4. Suppose that Condition A.2(ii) holds, and that p is nonlattice. Then there exist constants C ą 0 and c ą 0 such that, for all κ and all mκ with 0 ď mκ ď nκ, |φSmκptq| ď Ce´cmκp1´mκ{nκqt2, |t| ď π. (A.12) Proof. For convenience, we drop the subscripts κ. We use the conditioning method implicit in [12] (see also [17] and [21, Example 2.3]). Let ξi (1 ď i ď n) be i.i.d. Bernoulli random variables with Ppξi “ 1q “ p :“ m{n, (A.13) let ηi :“ diξi, and define the random vectors ζi :“ pξi, ηiq “ pξi, diξiq. Let Z “ pX, Y q :“ nÿ i“1 ζi. (A.14) Note that X “ řn i“1 ξi has a binomial distribution Bipn, pq. Moreover, it follows from the construction that Sm d“ pY | X “ mq. (A.15) Let φZps, tq :“ E eisX`itY be the characteristic 