 more than one fringe tree 24 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON of this size when |nκ| ą 2ℓ. We have πℓą 0 for all large ℓ, but the example above shows that πℓ“ 0 is possible for some small ℓ. △ Remark 5.4. We conjecture that when mκ ! |nκ|2{3, and thus ErNmκpTnκqs Ñ 8 by Lemma 5.1, we have asymptotic normality, in analogy with Theorems 3.2, 3.3, and 4.2. It might be possible to prove this using the Gao–Wormald theorem, as in the other theorems just mentioned, but since this would require estimates of moments of unbounded order, the estimates in the proof of Theorem 5.2 are not precise enough; we would need either a better estimate of the error in Theorem A.3, or a different, non-inductive, way to estimate (5.4) or (5.12). △ Remark 5.5. We have assumed in both Lemma 5.1 and Theorem 5.2 that the asymptotic degree distribution p is nonlattice. The results are easily extended to the case when all degrees diκ are divisible by some fixed integer h ą 1, for example if all degrees are even; we may then apply Theorem A.3 to the integers diκ{h. There are, however, also limiting cases. For example, suppose that p is concentrated on the even integers, and thus lattice, but that nκp1q ą 0 although nκp1q “ op|nκ|q. In Example 5.6 below, one example of this type is studied in some detail, and it is seen that in the critical case mκ “ Θp|nκ|2{3q, there will periodicities in the result if nκp1q “ Op|nκ|1{3q, but it seems that these disappear if nκp1q is larger. We believe that this behaviour is typical for cases with p lattice, but we have not pursued this further, and leave such cases to the reader. △ Example 5.6. Suppose that |nκ| Ñ 8 with nκpiq ą 0 only for i P t0, 1, 2u, and that nκp1q „ b|nκ|1{3 for some constant b ą 0. Then it follows from (2.3) that necessarily nκp0q “ 1 2p|nκ| ´ nκp1q ` 1q “ nκp2q ` 1, and thus Condition 2.1 holds with p0 “ p2 “ 1 2; hence p is concentrated on t0, 2u and thus has span 2. Condition 2.3 holds too. Let mκ „ a|nκ|2{3 for some a ą 0, let d be uniformly random in Bnκ, and let Yκ :“ |ti ď mκ : diκ “ 1u|. It is easy to see that as κ Ñ 8, Yκ d ÝÑ Y P Popabq. Conditioned on Yκ, the remaining mκ ´ Yκ degrees diκ with i ď mκ are all 0 or 2, so their sum is even, and the number of 2s has a hypergeometric distribution; it follows easily from this (for example using Theorem A.3) that for every fixed y P N0, PrSmκ “ mκ ´ 1 | Yκ “ ys “ # 0, y ı mκ ´ 1 pmod 2q, 2 ?2πmκ p1 ` op1qq, y ” mκ ´ 1 pmod 2q. (5.20) Furthermore, this holds uniformly for all y ď m1{2 κ , say, and it follows easily from (5.3) that, cf. (5.10) and Lemma 5.1(ii) in the nonlattice case, E NmκpTnκq “ |nκ| mκ PrSmκ “ mκ ´ 1 | Yκ “ ys „ 2|nκ| ? 2πmκ3 P ` Yκ ” mκ ´ 1 pmod 2q ˘ „ #1`e´ab ? 2πa3 , mκ is odd, 1´e´ab ? 2πa3 , mκ is even. (5.21) Higher factorial moments can be calculated asymptotically by the method in the proof of The- orem 5.2, and it follows that NmκpTnκq has one asymptotic Poisson distribution for even mκ, and a different asymptotic Poisson distribution for odd mκ. □ 6. Application to Galton–Watson Trees As in our previous paper [5] for the number of fringe trees equal to a fixed tree T (not depending on κ), we can use results for random trees with given vertex degrees to obtain corresponding results also for conditioned Galton–Watson trees (or the somewhat more general LARGE FRINGE TREES 25 simply generated trees) by conditioning on the degree statistic of the tree. For the results in the present paper, where we consider fringe trees Tκ growing with κ, such results for Galton–Watson trees have been shown by Cai and Devroye [8] by other methods. (See also [23] for the case of a fixed tree Tκ “ T.) We will here illustrate the conditioning method by giving alternative proofs of two of their results, in one case extending it to include also some offspring distributions with infinite variance. We consider for simplicity here a conditioned Galton–Watson tree Tp,n obtained from a Galton–Watson tree Tp with offspring distribution p having mean 1 by conditioning on the size |Tp| “ n; it is well known, using tiltings, that the results immediately extend to conditioned Galton–Watson trees with a large class of offspring distributions, and even further to a large class of simply generated trees, see e.g. [22]. The key fact is (as in the related arguments in [5]) that for any fixed degree statistic n “ pnpiqqiě0 with PpnTp,n “ nq ą 0, it follows from (2.23), see e.g. [1, Proposition 8], that conditionally given nTp,n “ n, we have Tp,n P UnifpTnq. (6.1) We begin with a straightforward conditioning argument applied to the number of fringe trees of a given size. The result is essentially a special case of [8, Theorem 1.4(ii)] (there proved directly by the method of moments), although we have also computed the (asymptotic) expectation. Theorem 6.1 ([8, Theorem 1.4(ii)]). Let p :“ ppiqiě0 be a probability distribution on N0 such that p0 ą 0 and pi ą 0 for at least one i ě 2. Let Tp,n be a Galton–Watson tree with offspring distribution p conditioned to have size n P N (we only consider n along values for which the conditioning is well defined). Furthermore, suppose that p is nonlattice, the mean ř iě0 ipi “ 1, and the variance σ2 p :“ ř iě0pi ´ 1q2pi ă 8. Let mn be integers with 1 ď mn ď n such that mn „ an2{3 as n Ñ 8, for some constant a ą 0. Then, as n Ñ 8, NmnpTp,nq d ÝÑ Po ` p2πσ2 pa3q´1{2˘ . (6.2) Proof. Let PpN0q be the set of probability measures on N0 equipped with the weak topology. By [6, Lemma 11], we have that, as n Ñ 8, ´ ppnTp,nq, σ2 ppnTp,nq ¯ d ÝÑ pp, σ2 pq, (6.3) where the convergence holds in the space PpN0qˆR equipped with the product topology. By the Skorohod coupling theorem [26, Theorem 4.30], we can and will assume that the convergence in (6.3) holds a.s.; in other words, Condition 2.1 and Condition 2.3 hold a.s. for the degree statistics nTp,n, with p. Moreover, e.g. by resampling Tp,n conditioned on nTp,n, we may assume that conditioned on the sequence of degree statistics pnTp,nq8 n“1, the random trees Tp,n, n ě 1, are independent. As noted above, for any fixed degree statistic n with PpnTp,n “ nq ą 0, we have (6.1). It follows that we may apply Theorem 5.2 conditioned on the sequence of degree statistics pnTp,nq8 n“1; this shows that (6.2) holds conditioned on pnTp,nq8 n“1. Then, (6.2) also holds unconditionally by the dominated convergence theorem. □ The same argument can be used to give versions for the conditioned Galton–Watson tree Tp,n of the Poisson convergence parts of Theorems 3.2 and 4.2. In principle, it should be possible to apply this method also to the parts with normal convergence, but then we would have to also take into account the random fluctuations of the degree statistic of the conditioned Galton– Watson tree Tp,n, see [5, Section 7] where this is done in detail for NT pTp,nq where T is a fixed tree. Instead we go back to the proofs above and use moment calculations there to show the 26 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON following result which includes [8, Theorem 1.3] (although without an explicit error bound) and extends it to some cases where p has infinite variance. Theorem 6.2 (Partly [8, Theorem 1.3]). Let p :“ ppiqiě0 be a probability distribution on N0 such that p0 ą 0 and pi ą 0 for at least one i ě 2. Let Tp,n be a Galton–Watson tree with offspring distribution p conditioned to have size n P N (we only consider n along values for which the conditioning is well defined). Furthermore, suppose that the mean ř iě0 ipi “ 1 and that p satisfies one of the following conditions: (a) σ2 p :“ ř iě0pi ´ 1q2pi P p0, 8q. (I.e., the distribution p has finite variance σ2.) (b) σ2 p “ 8 and p belongs to the domain of attraction of a stable law of index α P p1, 2s. (The last condition is equivalent to that there exists a slowly varying function L : R` Ñ R` such that řk i“0 i2pi “ k2´αLpkq, as k Ñ 8 [13, Theorem XVII.5.2].) Let Tn P T be such that mn :“ |Tn| Ñ 8. As n Ñ 8, (i) If nπppTnq