e pkpdκq Ñ pk, where p :“ ppkqkPZ is a probability distribution on Z. (iii) Qκ “ Opnκq. In other words, if Dκ is a random element of the sequence dκ, then Var Dκ “ Op1q. We can now state our local limit theorem. Theorem A.3. Let dκ (κ ě 1) be (finite) integer sequences such that, with notations as above, Condition A.2 holds with limit p nonlattice, and also the Lindeberg condition (A.7) holds. Then, as κ Ñ 8, uniformly for all k P Z, PpSmκ “ kq “ 1 a 2πpσ2κ ˆ exp ˆ ´pk ´ mκ ¯dκq2 2pσ2κ ˙ ` op1q ˙ . (A.11) 30 GABRIEL BERZUNZA OJEDA, CECILIA HOLMGREN, AND SVANTE JANSON The proof uses the standard method of Fourier inversion using a global limit theorem (Theo- rem A.1) combined with an estimate of the characteristic function away from 0 (and multiples of 2π). Note that both the proof of Theorem A.1 in [12] and the Berry–Esseen estimates in [19; 20] are based on estimates of the characteristic function, but they need only estimates for close to 0 (on the other hand, they need more precise estimates there). Note also that the assumption that p is nonlattice is necessary, since (A.11) cannot hold without modification if, for example, all diκ are even. We prove first a lemma with the required estimate of the characteristic function φSmκptq of Smκ. Lemma A.4. Suppose that Condition A.2(ii) holds, and that p is nonlattice. Then there exist constants C ą 0 and c ą 0 such that, for all κ and all mκ with 0 ď mκ ď nκ, |φSmκptq| ď Ce´cmκp1´mκ{nκqt2, |t| ď π. (A.12) Proof. For convenience, we drop the subscripts κ. We use the conditioning method implicit in [12] (see also [17] and [21, Example 2.3]). Let ξi (1 ď i ď n) be i.i.d. Bernoulli random variables with Ppξi “ 1q “ p :“ m{n, (A.13) let ηi :“ diξi, and define the random vectors ζi :“ pξi, ηiq “ pξi, diξiq. Let Z “ pX, Y q :“ nÿ i“1 ζi. (A.14) Note that X “ řn i“1 ξi has a binomial distribution Bipn, pq. Moreover, it follows from the construction that Sm d“ pY | X “ mq. (A.15) Let φZps, tq :“ E eisX`itY be the characteristic 