The direct effects of AI on optimal distance are the same as in the baseline model: higher
AI power facilitates more distant recombinations. However, the quality-ladder framework
introduces an additional, countervailing force. The aggregate replacement rate Λt+1, which
depends on both the number of competing firms Nt+1 and the probability that each succeeds,
appears in the denominator of the optimal distance expression. When Λt+1 is high, either
because there are many competitors or because competitors target shorter (and therefore
more likely to succeed) recombinations, the expected duration of the innovation monopoly
shrinks. This reduces the expected reward from distant recombinations and pushes R&D
firms toward shorter and safer targets.
