5. Limitations
We note two key limitations of MOSAIC. First, the relax-
ation from Equation 2 to Equation 3 assumes that each
cluster’s contribution is well captured by its own scaling
curve ∆Ui(n), with limited cross-cluster interactions. Con-
sequently, if the clustering fails to produce well-separated
groups, this assumption may be violated, leading MOSAIC
to suboptimal allocation.
Second, MOSAIC relies on pilot runs to estimate cluster-
specific scaling curves, which introduces additional com-
putational cost. However, as shown in Section 9 of the Ap-
pendix, accurate scaling fits can be obtained efficiently us-
