We also note that the discrepancy between the Actual
and Estimated are accumulation of two factors: i) the cross-
cluster interactions, ii) extrapolation errors of the scaling
fits. Hence, Table 5 should be interpreted as an upper bound
on interaction effects rather than a pure estimate thereof.
Also, as a contrasting example, if clusters were formed
randomly and lacked semantic coherence, cross-cluster in-
teractions would likely be large, and the approximation
would break down. In such pathological settings, explicitly
modeling interaction terms would be necessary for optimal
data selection.
